// Arts and Culture: science fiction, detective fiction, origami and graphic design.
ADDART({id:"scifi",t:"Science Fiction",c:"Literature",i:"🚀",w:"Science fiction",r:["novel","robot","ai","space","phlit","wormholes"],
p:[["What if?","Science fiction imagines how science and technology might change the world."],["Stories of the future","It explores space travel, robots, time travel and alien life."],["Inspiring inventors","Some sci-fi ideas later became real technologies."]],
l:"Science fiction is a genre of stories that imagine how science, technology and possible future events might change human life and society. It includes tales of space travel, time travel, robots, artificial intelligence, alien civilizations, future cities and alternate histories. Many consider Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein, published in 1818, an early science fiction novel. Writers such as Jules Verne and H. G. Wells in the 1800s, and later Isaac Asimov, Arthur C. Clarke, Ursula K. Le Guin, Octavia Butler and Liu Cixin, made the genre popular around the world. Science fiction appears in novels, short stories, comics, films, television and games. It is entertaining, but it also helps people think about the benefits and dangers of new technologies. Filipino writers have built a growing tradition of speculative fiction.",
s:[["Early works","Before the modern genre, ancient and medieval stories imagined journeys to the Moon and mechanical people. Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein, about a scientist who creates life, raised questions about the responsibilities of science. In the 1800s, Jules Verne wrote adventures such as Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas, and H. G. Wells wrote The Time Machine and The War of the Worlds."],["The golden age","From the 1930s to the 1950s, magazines in the United States published many science fiction stories, and writers such as Isaac Asimov, Robert Heinlein and Arthur C. Clarke became famous. Asimov’s stories introduced his Three Laws of Robotics, imagining rules to make robots safe."],["New voices","From the 1960s onward, science fiction explored social issues, gender, race, ecology and politics. Ursula K. Le Guin imagined societies without fixed genders, and Octavia Butler wrote about race, power and survival. Writers from Africa, Latin America and Asia have brought new perspectives, and Liu Cixin’s The Three-Body Problem became the first Asian novel to win the Hugo Award for Best Novel in 2015."],["Common themes","Science fiction often explores what it means to be human, through stories of robots, clones and artificial intelligence. It imagines first contact with aliens, life in space, environmental disasters and future governments. Dystopian stories, which show oppressive future societies, warn about dangers such as surveillance and loss of freedom."]],
k:[["Early landmark","Frankenstein, 1818"],["19th-century pioneers","Jules Verne, H. G. Wells"],["Famous robot rules","Asimov’s Three Laws"],["Major award","Hugo Award"],["Related term","Speculative fiction"]],
d:[["1818","Mary Shelley publishes Frankenstein"],["1870","Jules Verne publishes Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas"],["1898","H. G. Wells publishes The War of the Worlds"],["1938","A radio drama of The War of the Worlds is broadcast in the United States"],["1942","Isaac Asimov introduces the Three Laws of Robotics"],["2015","Liu Cixin wins the Hugo Award"]],
s2:[["Philippine speculative fiction","Filipino writers have long written tales of the fantastic, drawing on folklore of aswang, diwata and engkanto. Since 2005 a series of anthologies called Philippine Speculative Fiction has published science fiction, fantasy and horror stories by Filipino authors in English and Filipino. Komiks, such as the works of Mars Ravelo, also featured superheroes and fantastic adventures."],["Predicting the future","Some science fiction ideas foreshadowed real inventions. Verne imagined electric submarines, and in 1945 Arthur C. Clarke described communication satellites in orbits that keep them above one spot, now called geostationary orbits. Writers also imagined video calls, tablets and artificial intelligence. However, science fiction is not mainly about prediction; many ideas never came true, and others came in unexpected forms."],["The War of the Worlds broadcast","On October 30, 1938, the American radio program The Mercury Theatre on the Air broadcast an adaptation of The War of the Worlds as realistic news bulletins about a Martian invasion. Newspapers reported widespread panic. Later historians argue that the panic was much smaller than reported, but the broadcast remains a famous example of the power of media."],["Science fiction and science","Many scientists and engineers say science fiction inspired them to pursue their careers. Space agencies have consulted writers, and some companies hire science fiction writers to imagine future products. Some critics warn that science fiction can also make people expect technologies that may never be possible or overlook real-world problems."],["On screen","Science fiction films and television series, from early silent films to modern blockbusters, have been hugely popular, using special effects to show alien worlds and future cities. Many explore the same themes as novels: artificial intelligence, space exploration, time travel and the future of humanity."],["Climate fiction","A growing branch of science fiction, sometimes called climate fiction or cli-fi, imagines how climate change might reshape societies. These stories explore flooded cities, migration, new technologies and ways people might adapt, inviting readers to think about choices made today."]],
sp:["A ghost story contest","In the summer of 1816, the year without a summer caused by a volcanic eruption in Indonesia, a group of young writers was stuck indoors by stormy weather at a villa near Lake Geneva in Switzerland. The poet Lord Byron suggested that each of them write a ghost story. Eighteen-year-old Mary Godwin, soon to marry the poet Percy Shelley, struggled for days to think of an idea. Then, she later wrote, she had a vivid waking dream of a scientist kneeling beside a creature he had brought to life. That vision became Frankenstein, published in 1818. Many consider it one of the first science fiction novels, and its questions about science and responsibility still resonate."],
g:[["Science fiction","Stories about imagined science and technology"],["Speculative fiction","A broad term for science fiction, fantasy and related genres"],["Dystopia","An imagined society that is oppressive or frightening"],["Hugo Award","A major award for science fiction"]],
f:["Frankenstein was published in 1818.","Asimov created the Three Laws of Robotics.","Liu Cixin won the Hugo Award in 2015."],
fun:"Arthur C. Clarke described communication satellites in 1945, years before the first satellite was launched."});

ADDART({id:"detective",t:"Detective and Mystery Fiction",c:"Literature",i:"🔍",w:"Detective fiction",r:["novel","law","shakes","phlit","logic"],
p:[["Solving crimes","Detective stories follow investigators solving mysteries."],["Famous detectives","Characters like Sherlock Holmes became world-famous."],["Clues and twists","Readers try to solve the puzzle before the detective."]],
l:"Detective fiction is a genre in which a detective, whether a police officer, private investigator or amateur, investigates a crime, usually a murder, and reveals who committed it and how. Mystery stories challenge readers to follow clues and solve the puzzle along with, or before, the detective. The American writer Edgar Allan Poe is often credited with creating the modern detective story with The Murders in the Rue Morgue in 1841. Arthur Conan Doyle’s Sherlock Holmes and Agatha Christie’s Hercule Poirot and Miss Marple became among the most famous fictional characters ever. The genre includes cozy mysteries, hard-boiled crime novels, police procedurals and Scandinavian noir, and has spread worldwide. In the Philippines, F. H. Batacan’s Smaller and Smaller Circles is celebrated as a pioneering Filipino crime novel.",
s:[["Poe’s detective","In The Murders in the Rue Morgue, Poe introduced C. Auguste Dupin, a brilliant amateur who solves a baffling double murder in Paris through careful reasoning. Poe’s stories established many features of the genre: the genius detective, a friend who narrates the story, the locked-room mystery and the final explanation of the solution."],["Sherlock Holmes","Arthur Conan Doyle’s detective Sherlock Holmes first appeared in A Study in Scarlet in 1887. Holmes, who lives at 221B Baker Street in London with his friend Dr. Watson, solves cases through observation and deduction, noticing tiny details others miss. The stories became so popular that when Doyle killed Holmes off in 1893, public outcry eventually led him to bring the detective back."],["The golden age","In the 1920s and 1930s, writers such as Agatha Christie, Dorothy L. Sayers and Ellery Queen wrote puzzle mysteries with clever plots and fair clues. Christie’s novels, such as Murder on the Orient Express and And Then There Were None, made her one of the best-selling novelists in history."],["Hard-boiled and beyond","In the United States, writers such as Dashiell Hammett and Raymond Chandler created tough private detectives in gritty cities, a style called hard-boiled. Later forms include police procedurals, which follow realistic police work, psychological thrillers and Nordic noir from Scandinavia. Japanese writers developed a popular tradition of puzzle mysteries called honkaku."]],
k:[["Pioneer","Edgar Allan Poe, 1841"],["Most famous detective","Sherlock Holmes"],["Best-selling author","Agatha Christie"],["Classic puzzle","Locked-room mystery"],["Filipino landmark","Smaller and Smaller Circles"]],
d:[["1841","Poe publishes The Murders in the Rue Morgue"],["1868","Wilkie Collins publishes The Moonstone"],["1887","Sherlock Holmes first appears"],["1920","Agatha Christie publishes her first novel"],["1930","The Detection Club is founded in London"],["2002","F. H. Batacan publishes Smaller and Smaller Circles"]],
s2:[["A Filipino crime novel","Smaller and Smaller Circles by F. H. Batacan, first published in 2002, follows two Jesuit priests who are also forensic experts as they investigate a series of murders in a poor community in Metro Manila. Often described as the first Filipino crime novel, it won national literary awards and was adapted into a film in 2017. It explores poverty, corruption and the justice system alongside the mystery."],["Fair play rules","In 1929 the writer and priest Ronald Knox published ten rules for detective fiction, sometimes called Knox’s Decalogue, such as that the criminal must be mentioned early and that no undiscovered poisons or secret passages should be used without fair warning. Golden age writers saw mysteries as puzzles in which readers should have a fair chance to solve the crime."],["Agatha Christie","Agatha Christie wrote 66 detective novels and many short stories and plays. Her play The Mousetrap opened in London in 1952 and has run continuously for decades, the longest-running play in history apart from pandemic closures. In 1926 she herself disappeared for 11 days, causing a nationwide search, before being found at a hotel; the reasons remain debated."],["Science and detection","Detective fiction and real forensic science have influenced each other. Holmes used chemistry, footprints and tobacco ash to solve cases at a time when police rarely used science. Today, popular television dramas about forensic investigators have increased public interest in forensic science, though experts say they often exaggerate how quickly and certainly evidence can be analyzed."],["Mysteries around the world","Detective stories are popular worldwide. Japanese author Edogawa Ranpo, whose pen name echoes Edgar Allan Poe, founded a Japanese mystery tradition. China has a long tradition of crime stories about wise judges, such as Judge Bao and Judge Dee. Today, mystery novels and television series from many countries are translated and enjoyed globally."],["Why we love mysteries","Psychologists and critics suggest that mysteries appeal because they offer puzzles to solve, suspense, and the satisfaction of order being restored when the truth is revealed. They also let readers explore danger and moral questions safely."]],
sp:["The detective who would not die","By 1893 Arthur Conan Doyle was tired of Sherlock Holmes. He wanted to write historical novels he considered more serious. So in a story called The Final Problem, he sent Holmes to struggle with his archenemy, Professor Moriarty, at the Reichenbach Falls in Switzerland, where both apparently plunged to their deaths. Readers were furious. Thousands canceled subscriptions to the magazine that published the stories, and some reportedly wore black armbands in mourning. For years, Doyle resisted, but eventually, in 1903, he brought Holmes back in The Adventure of the Empty House, explaining that he had survived. Holmes’s popularity has never faded."],
g:[["Detective","A person who investigates crimes"],["Clue","A piece of evidence that helps solve a mystery"],["Deduction","Reasoning from clues to a conclusion"],["Locked-room mystery","A crime that seems impossible"]],
f:["Poe wrote an early detective story in 1841.","Sherlock Holmes first appeared in 1887.","Agatha Christie is one of the best-selling novelists ever."],
fun:"When Arthur Conan Doyle killed off Sherlock Holmes, readers were so upset that he eventually brought him back."});

ADDART({id:"origami",t:"Origami",c:"Art",i:"🦢",w:"Origami",r:["paper","shapes","japanese","euclid","robot"],
p:[["Folding paper","Origami is the art of folding paper into shapes."],["The paper crane","The folded crane is a symbol of peace and hope."],["Science of folding","Origami ideas are used in space, medicine and engineering."]],
l:"Origami is the art of folding paper into shapes, from simple boats and planes to elaborate animals, flowers and geometric forms, usually from a single square sheet without cutting or gluing. The word comes from Japanese ori, meaning folding, and kami, meaning paper. Paper folding developed in Japan and China after paper became more widely available, and similar traditions arose in Europe. In the 20th century, Japanese master Akira Yoshizawa developed new techniques and a system of diagrams that allowed origami to spread worldwide. The folded paper crane has become a symbol of peace, especially through the story of Sadako Sasaki of Hiroshima. Today mathematicians and engineers use origami principles to design solar panels for satellites, medical devices and folding robots.",
s:[["Basic folds","Origami uses a small number of basic folds, such as the valley fold, which folds paper toward you, and the mountain fold, which folds it away. Combined, they create bases, such as the bird base, from which many models are made. Diagrams with standard symbols show each step."],["Traditional models","Classic models include the crane, frog, boat, cup and jumping frog. Many children learn simple folds such as paper airplanes and boats. In Japan, origami is used in ceremonies, decorations and gift wrapping, and the paper crane is among the most famous designs."],["Modern origami","Modern origami artists create astonishingly detailed models, such as insects with legs and antennae, dragons and human figures, sometimes using computer programs to design crease patterns. Wet folding, developed by Yoshizawa, involves slightly dampening thick paper to create curved, lifelike shapes."],["Modular origami","Modular origami joins many identical folded units without glue to create complex shapes such as stars, balls and polyhedra. Some designs use dozens or hundreds of units. Teachers use modular origami to teach geometry."]],
k:[["Meaning","Folding paper"],["Origin","Japan, with related traditions elsewhere"],["Famous model","Paper crane"],["Modern master","Akira Yoshizawa"],["Engineering use","Folding solar panels"]],
d:[["1600s","Paper folding appears in Japanese records and art"],["1797","A Japanese book on folding paper cranes is published"],["1954","Akira Yoshizawa publishes a diagram system for origami"],["1955","Sadako Sasaki folds paper cranes in Hiroshima"],["1995","A Japanese satellite uses a solar panel based on the Miura fold"],["2010s","Engineers design folding robots inspired by origami"]],
s2:[["Sadako and the thousand cranes","Sadako Sasaki was two years old when the atomic bomb was dropped on Hiroshima in 1945. At age 12, she developed leukemia, linked to radiation. Inspired by a Japanese legend that folding a thousand paper cranes grants a wish, she folded cranes in the hospital until her death in 1955. Her story inspired the Children’s Peace Monument in Hiroshima, where people from around the world leave strings of paper cranes."],["Akira Yoshizawa","Akira Yoshizawa, who lived from 1911 to 2005, is often called the father of modern origami. He created tens of thousands of models and developed a system of arrows and dashed lines for diagrams, published in 1954, which became the international standard. He exhibited his work around the world and served as a cultural ambassador for Japan."],["Origami in space","In the 1970s the Japanese astrophysicist Koryo Miura invented a way of folding a sheet so that it can be opened and closed in one motion, now called the Miura fold. It was used to deploy solar panels on a Japanese space experiment in 1995. Engineers also use origami principles to fold large telescope mirrors, antennas and sunshields into rockets."],["Medicine and engineering","Origami-inspired designs are used in heart stents that fold small and expand inside blood vessels, car airbags that unfold quickly, folding furniture and shelters for disaster areas. Researchers have created tiny folding robots that can walk, swim or carry medicine."],["Mathematics of folding","Mathematicians study which shapes can be folded from a flat sheet and how many folds are needed. In 2002 a high school student, Britney Gallivan, showed that it is possible to fold a long strip of paper in half 12 times, breaking a popular belief that paper could only be folded about 7 times."],["Origami in schools","In the Philippines and many other countries, teachers use paper folding to teach geometry, patience and fine motor skills. Students fold paper cranes, frogs and boxes in art classes, and some schools hold origami clubs and exhibits."]],
sp:["Folding paper twelve times","For years, people repeated a popular claim: no matter how big or thin a sheet of paper is, you cannot fold it in half more than seven or eight times. In 2001 Britney Gallivan, a high school student in California, decided to test it. She worked out a mathematical formula for how long a strip of paper must be to fold it a given number of times, since each fold doubles the thickness. She then obtained a roll of thin paper about 1.2 kilometers long. With her family’s help, she spent hours carefully folding it in half again and again in a shopping mall. In January 2002 she reached 12 folds, setting a record and showing how mathematics can overturn common beliefs."],
g:[["Origami","The art of folding paper"],["Valley fold","A fold toward the viewer"],["Crease pattern","The lines of folds on an unfolded sheet"],["Miura fold","A folding pattern used in space engineering"]],
f:["Origami means folding paper in Japanese.","The paper crane is a symbol of peace.","Origami ideas are used in space technology."],
fun:"A student once folded a 1.2-kilometer strip of paper in half 12 times."});

ADDART({id:"graphicdesign",t:"Graphic Design",c:"Art",i:"🎨",w:"Graphic design",r:["print","calligraphy","jeepney","streetart","socialmedia","artnouveau"],
p:[["Visual communication","Graphic design combines images and words to communicate ideas."],["All around us","Logos, posters, signs, books and websites are all designed."],["Street-level design","Philippine jeepneys show a vibrant tradition of popular design."]],
l:"Graphic design is the art and practice of combining text, images, colors and shapes to communicate information and ideas visually. Graphic designers create logos, posters, book covers, magazines, packaging, signs, maps, websites, apps and advertisements. Good design makes messages clear, attractive and easy to understand. The field has roots in ancient writing and printing, and grew rapidly with the printing press and later with industrial advertising in the 1800s. Movements such as the Bauhaus in Germany and the Swiss International Style shaped modern design, while computers transformed design work from the 1980s. In the Philippines, graphic design can be seen everywhere, from colorful jeepney lettering and hand-painted signs to modern branding and digital media.",
s:[["Typography","Typography is the art of arranging letters. Designers choose typefaces, sizes and spacing to make text readable and to set a mood. Serif typefaces, with small strokes at the ends of letters, are often used in books, while sans-serif typefaces, without them, are common on screens and signs."],["Color and layout","Designers use color to attract attention, organize information and create emotions. Layout arranges elements on a page or screen so that viewers know where to look first. Principles such as contrast, alignment, repetition and white space help create clear designs."],["Logos and branding","A logo is a symbol representing a company, organization or product. Branding includes logos, colors, typefaces and styles used consistently to create an identity. Simple, memorable logos are easy to recognize even when small."],["Information design","Designers create maps, charts, signs and diagrams that help people understand complex information. The London Underground map, designed by Harry Beck in 1933, simplified the subway network into straight lines and evenly spaced stations, inspiring transit maps around the world, including in Metro Manila."]],
k:[["Main elements","Text, images, color, layout"],["Typeface types","Serif and sans-serif"],["Influential school","Bauhaus, 1919"],["Famous typeface","Helvetica, 1957"],["Philippine popular design","Jeepney lettering and signs"]],
d:[["c. 1450","Gutenberg’s printing press spreads printed design"],["1890s","Colorful posters by artists such as Alphonse Mucha flourish"],["1919","The Bauhaus school is founded in Germany"],["1933","Harry Beck’s London Underground map is published"],["1957","The Helvetica typeface is designed"],["1980s","Desktop computers transform graphic design"]],
s2:[["The Bauhaus","The Bauhaus, a German school of art and design founded in 1919, aimed to unite art, craft and industry. Its teachers and students developed a clean, functional style using simple shapes, bold colors and sans-serif typefaces. The school was closed in 1933 under pressure from the Nazi government, but its ideas spread worldwide as teachers emigrated."],["Jeepney art","Philippine jeepneys are famous for their bright paint, chrome decorations and hand-lettered names, slogans and route signs. Painters and sign makers created a distinctive style of lettering, images of horses, saints, landscapes and pop culture figures. This vernacular design has influenced Filipino graphic designers, though the modernization of public transport has raised questions about its future."],["Hand-painted signs","Before digital printing, Filipino sign painters hand-lettered signs for sari-sari stores, carinderias, cinemas and buses. Movie billboards in Manila were once painted by hand. Many sign painters lost work as printed tarpaulins became cheap, but some designers now celebrate and revive their craft."],["Designing money","Banknotes and coins are carefully designed to show national identity and to prevent counterfeiting. The Philippines’ New Generation Currency banknotes, introduced from 2010, feature national heroes, natural wonders and endemic species, along with security features. In 2022 the central bank began issuing polymer banknotes featuring the Philippine eagle and other wildlife, a change that drew both praise and criticism."],["Design and computers","In the 1980s, personal computers, laser printers and desktop publishing software allowed designers to create layouts on screens instead of by hand. Today designers use digital tools for print, web, apps and motion graphics, and online platforms allow non-professionals to create designs."],["Design and accessibility","Good design considers everyone, including people with visual impairments or color blindness. Designers use high contrast, readable fonts, clear icons and alternative text for images. Accessible design makes information easier for all users to understand."]],
sp:["A map like a circuit","In 1931 Harry Beck, a young engineering draftsman working for the London Underground, had an idea while sketching in his spare time. The existing maps showed train lines following their real twisting paths, crowding stations in the center and leaving the outskirts spread out. Beck reasoned that passengers underground did not need exact geography; they needed to know the order of stations and where to change lines. Inspired partly by electrical circuit diagrams, he drew lines only horizontally, vertically or at 45 degrees, with stations evenly spaced. At first, officials thought it too radical, but a trial printing in 1933 was a huge success. His design became the model for transit maps around the world."],
g:[["Graphic design","Visual communication using text and images"],["Typography","The art of arranging letters"],["Logo","A symbol representing an organization"],["Layout","The arrangement of elements on a page"]],
f:["The Bauhaus was founded in 1919.","Harry Beck designed the London Underground map.","Jeepney lettering is a Filipino design tradition."],
fun:"Harry Beck’s famous subway map was inspired partly by electrical circuit diagrams."});

;
