// Articles for thin sub-sections, part 1: The Modern World (the United Nations, decolonization, ASEAN).
ADDART({id:"un",t:"The United Nations",c:"History",i:"🕊️",w:"United Nations",r:["ww2","coldwar","decol","imfworldbank","redcross","g20brics","philanthropy","climateactivism","landmines"],
p:[["A world organization","The United Nations is an organization of 193 countries that work together on peace, human rights and development."],["Born after war","It was founded in 1945, at the end of World War II, to prevent another world war."],["A founding member","51 countries founded the UN in 1945; today it has 193 members."]],
l:"The United Nations, or UN, is the world’s largest international organization, with 193 member countries. Founded in 1945 after the destruction of World War II, it aims to keep peace, protect human rights, deliver aid and help countries develop. Its headquarters is in New York, and its agencies work in almost every country on Earth.",
s:[["Founding","Delegates from 50 countries met in San Francisco in 1945 to write the Charter of the United Nations, and Poland signed soon after as the 51st founding member. The Charter came into force on 24 October 1945, now celebrated as United Nations Day. The UN replaced the League of Nations, which had failed to stop World War II."],["The General Assembly","Every member country has one seat and one vote in the General Assembly, which meets in New York each September. Leaders give speeches and the Assembly votes on resolutions about world problems. Its decisions are recommendations; countries are not forced to follow them."],["The Security Council","The Security Council is responsible for international peace. It has 15 members. Five permanent members, China, France, Russia, the United Kingdom and the United States, can each block a decision with a veto. The Council can send peacekeepers and impose sanctions."],["The Secretary-General","The Secretary-General leads the UN’s staff and speaks for the organization. As of 2026 the post is held by António Guterres of Portugal, whose second term ends on 31 December 2026. The only Asian holders so far have been U Thant of Burma and Ban Ki-moon of South Korea."]],
k:[["Founded","24 October 1945"],["Members","193 countries"],["Headquarters","New York City"],["Official languages","6"],["Founding members","51"]],
d:[["1920","The League of Nations, the UN’s forerunner, is founded"],["1945","The UN Charter comes into force on 24 October"],["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights is adopted"],["1946","The first General Assembly meets in London"],["2015","The UN adopts 17 Sustainable Development Goals"]],
s2:[["Human rights","On 10 December 1948, the General Assembly adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, which says that all people are born free and equal in dignity and rights. It has been translated into more than 500 languages, more than any other document, and has inspired many national constitutions."],["Peacekeeping","UN peacekeepers, known as Blue Helmets, are soldiers and police lent by member countries to help keep ceasefires and protect civilians. Missions have served in places such as Cyprus, Lebanon and South Sudan. UN peacekeepers won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1988. Countries such as Bangladesh, Nepal, India and Rwanda are among the largest contributors of troops."],["Agencies","Much of the UN’s work is done by its agencies and programs. UNICEF helps children, the World Health Organization fights disease, UNESCO protects education, science and heritage sites, and the World Food Programme delivers food in emergencies and won the 2020 Nobel Peace Prize."],["UN cities","The UN’s headquarters stands beside the East River in New York, on land that is international territory. It also has major offices in Geneva, Vienna and Nairobi, and its agencies are spread around the world: the Food and Agriculture Organization in Rome, UNESCO in Paris, the World Health Organization in Geneva and the UN Environment Programme in Nairobi."],["Successes and criticism","The UN has helped end conflicts, wipe out smallpox, protect refugees and feed millions. Critics say it can be slow, and that the veto lets the five permanent members block action, even during wars. Many countries want the Security Council reformed to better reflect today’s world."],["Global goals","In 2015 all UN members agreed on 17 Sustainable Development Goals to reach by 2030, including ending poverty and hunger, providing quality education, achieving gender equality, providing clean water and taking action on climate change. Progress is measured each year."]],
sp:["Dag Hammarskjöld","Dag Hammarskjöld of Sweden, Secretary-General from 1953 to 1961, shaped the job into what it is today. He sent the first large UN peacekeeping force to Egypt in 1956 and traveled tirelessly to calm crises. In 1961 he died in a plane crash in what is now Zambia while trying to end fighting in the Congo, and he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize after his death."],
g:[["Charter","The founding agreement of an organization"],["Veto","The power to block a decision"],["Peacekeeper","A soldier sent to help keep peace"],["Resolution","A formal decision voted on by a group"]],
f:["United Nations Day is celebrated every 24 October.","The UN has six official languages: Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish.","The first General Assembly met in London in January 1946."],
fun:"The UN has six official languages: Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish."});

ADDART({id:"decol",t:"Decolonization and New Nations",c:"History",i:"🗺️",w:"Decolonization",r:["independence","un","coldwar"],
p:[["The end of empires","After World War II, colonies across Asia and Africa won their independence from European empires and the United States."],["Dozens of new countries","Between 1945 and 1975, more than 80 countries became independent."],["Asia leads the way","In the first years after the war, the Philippines, India, Pakistan, Burma and Sri Lanka became independent."]],
l:"Decolonization is the process by which colonies became independent nations. In the decades after World War II, the great European empires that had ruled much of Asia, Africa and the Caribbean broke apart. Some countries won freedom through negotiation, others through long struggles and wars. The map of the world, and the membership of the United Nations, changed completely.",
s:[["Why empires ended","World War II weakened Britain, France and the Netherlands, while Japan’s wartime conquests showed that European rule could be broken. Independence movements had been growing for decades. The new United Nations supported self-determination, the right of peoples to choose their own government, and the United States and Soviet Union both opposed old-style empires."],["Asia","The Philippines gained independence in 1946. India and Pakistan followed in 1947, Burma and Sri Lanka in 1948, and Indonesia won recognition from the Netherlands in 1949 after four years of war. Malaya became independent in 1957. Vietnam fought France until 1954 and then faced a long war that lasted until 1975."],["Africa","Ghana, led by Kwame Nkrumah, became the first colony in sub-Saharan Africa to win independence, in 1957. In 1960 alone, 17 African countries became independent, and it was called the Year of Africa. Algeria won independence from France in 1962 after a bitter eight-year war."],["Hard beginnings","New nations often inherited borders drawn by colonial powers that cut across peoples and languages. The partition of India and Pakistan in 1947 led to violence that killed hundreds of thousands and forced millions to flee. Many new countries struggled with poverty, civil wars and military coups in their early years."]],
k:[["Main period","About 1945–1975"],["New countries","More than 80"],["UN members in 1945","51"],["UN members today","193"],["Year of Africa","1960, when 17 African countries became independent"]],
d:[["1945","Indonesia declares independence"],["1947","India and Pakistan become independent"],["1955","Asian and African leaders meet at the Bandung Conference"],["1960","The Year of Africa: 17 African countries become independent"],["2002","Timor-Leste becomes independent, the first new country of the 21st century"]],
s2:[["The Bandung Conference","In 1955 leaders of 29 Asian and African countries met in Bandung, Indonesia, to promote cooperation and oppose colonialism. Leaders such as Sukarno of Indonesia, Jawaharlal Nehru of India, Zhou Enlai of China and Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt took part. The meeting helped inspire the Non-Aligned Movement, founded in 1961 by countries that wanted to stay out of the Cold War."],["Gandhi and nonviolence","In India, Mahatma Gandhi led mass campaigns of nonviolent resistance, such as the Salt March of 1930, against British rule. His methods inspired movements for freedom and civil rights around the world, including Martin Luther King Jr. in the United States and Nelson Mandela in South Africa."],["Wars of independence","Not all independence came peacefully. Indonesia fought the Netherlands, Vietnam fought France, Algeria fought France, and Angola and Mozambique fought Portugal until 1975. These wars caused great suffering, and their memories still shape the countries today."],["The United Nations grows","As new countries joined, the UN grew from 51 members in 1945 to 127 by 1970. In 1960 the General Assembly declared that colonialism should be brought to a speedy end. Today the UN still lists 17 non-self-governing territories, such as Western Sahara and some small islands."],["Legacies","Former colonies often keep the language, laws and sports of their colonizers. English is widely used in the Philippines, India and Nigeria, and French in much of West Africa. Some countries kept close ties through groups such as the Commonwealth of Nations, while others broke away completely."],["Southeast Asia’s new nations","Southeast Asia changed from a region of colonies to a region of independent nations within about 40 years. Brunei became fully independent in 1984, and Timor-Leste, after Portuguese rule and Indonesian occupation, became independent in 2002. Many of these countries later joined together in ASEAN."]],
sp:["1960, the Year of Africa","In 1960 alone, 17 African countries won independence, including Nigeria, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Senegal, Madagascar and Somalia, most of them former French colonies. That December the UN General Assembly declared that all colonized peoples had the right to independence. Within a few years most of Africa was free of colonial rule, though Portugal’s colonies had to fight until 1975."],
g:[["Colony","A land ruled by another country"],["Decolonization","The process of colonies becoming independent"],["Self-determination","The right of a people to choose its own government"],["Partition","Dividing a country into separate parts"]],
f:["In 1960 alone, 17 African countries became independent.","The UN grew from 51 members in 1945 to 193 today.","Timor-Leste became independent in 2002."],
fun:"In 1960, the Year of Africa, 17 African countries became independent."});

ADDART({id:"asean",t:"ASEAN and Modern Southeast Asia",c:"History",i:"🤝",w:"ASEAN",r:["sea","decol","un"],
p:[["A regional family","ASEAN, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, links the countries of Southeast Asia."],["Founded in 1967","Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand founded it."],["Many peoples","ASEAN countries are home to about 680 million people speaking hundreds of languages."]],
l:"The Association of Southeast Asian Nations, or ASEAN, is a regional organization that brings together the countries of Southeast Asia to promote peace, trade and cooperation. Founded in 1967 by five countries, it now has 11 members and is one of the world’s most important economic regions.",
s:[["Founding","ASEAN was founded on 8 August 1967 when the foreign ministers of Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand, Adam Malik, Tun Abdul Razak, Narciso Ramos, S. Rajaratnam and Thanat Khoman, signed the Bangkok Declaration. At the time, the region faced the Vietnam War and disputes among neighbors, and the founders wanted to build trust."],["Growing membership","Brunei joined in 1984, Vietnam in 1995, Laos and Myanmar in 1997, and Cambodia in 1999, making ten members. Timor-Leste was admitted as the eleventh member in 2025. ASEAN’s secretariat is in Jakarta, Indonesia."],["The ASEAN Way","ASEAN members make decisions by consultation and consensus, meaning all must agree. They promise not to interfere in each other’s internal affairs. Supporters say this has kept peace among members; critics say it makes ASEAN slow to act on problems such as human rights abuses."],["Trade and travel","ASEAN countries have cut tariffs on most goods traded among them and formed the ASEAN Economic Community in 2015. Citizens of member countries can visit one another for short stays without visas. Together their economies are among the largest in the world."]],
k:[["Founded","8 August 1967, Bangkok"],["Members","11"],["Population","About 680 million"],["Secretariat","Jakarta, Indonesia"],["Motto","One Vision, One Identity, One Community"]],
d:[["1967","Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand found ASEAN"],["1976","ASEAN leaders sign the Treaty of Amity and Cooperation"],["1999","Cambodia joins, making ten members"],["2015","The ASEAN Economic Community is launched"],["2025","Timor-Leste becomes ASEAN’s eleventh member"]],
s2:[["Southeast Asia today","Southeast Asia includes mainland countries such as Thailand, Vietnam, Myanmar, Laos and Cambodia, and island nations such as the Philippines, Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Brunei and Timor-Leste. Its cities, including Manila, Jakarta and Bangkok, are among the fastest-growing in the world."],["Rising economies","Since the 1980s, many Southeast Asian countries have grown rapidly by making electronics, clothes and cars for export, growing food and welcoming tourists. Singapore became one of the richest countries in the world. Vietnam, Indonesia and the Philippines have large, young populations."],["The South China Sea","Several ASEAN members, including Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia and Brunei, have overlapping claims with China in the South China Sea. In 2016 an international tribunal, in a case brought by the Philippines, ruled that China’s claims to historic rights over most of the sea had no legal basis, but China rejected the ruling. ASEAN and China have long negotiated a code of conduct."],["Disasters and cooperation","Southeast Asia faces typhoons, earthquakes, volcanoes and floods. ASEAN set up a center in Jakarta to coordinate disaster relief, which helped after Typhoon Haiyan, or Yolanda, struck the Philippines in 2013. Members also cooperate on fighting disease, crime and haze from forest fires."],["Shared culture","Southeast Asian countries share rice-based cooking, island and river trade and influences from India, China, Islam and Europe. Many share similar words, such as kanin and nasi for cooked rice. The Southeast Asian Games, held every two years, bring athletes from all members together."],["Chairing ASEAN","The chair of ASEAN rotates every year among the members, usually in alphabetical order. The chairing country hosts the summits, where leaders of the member states meet with partners such as China, Japan, South Korea, India, Australia and the United States. Each chair sets a theme for its year."]],
sp:["The ASEAN flag","The ASEAN flag shows ten bundles of rice stalks tied together, standing for the founding dream of all Southeast Asian countries bound in friendship. The blue stands for peace and stability, red for courage, white for purity and yellow for prosperity. The flag was adopted in 1997, before Timor-Leste joined."],
g:[["Consensus","Agreement by everyone in a group"],["Tariff","A tax on goods brought in from another country"],["Secretariat","The main office of an organization"],["Arbitral tribunal","A panel of judges that settles a dispute"]],
f:["ASEAN was founded in Bangkok in 1967.","The ASEAN flag shows bundled rice stalks.","Timor-Leste became ASEAN’s eleventh member in 2025."],
fun:"The ASEAN flag shows a bundle of rice stalks tied together."});

;
/* data/more2.js */
// Articles for thin sub-sections, part 2: Explorers (Zheng He, Ibn Battuta, Christopher Columbus).
ADDART({id:"zhenghe",t:"Zheng He",c:"People",i:"⛵",w:"Zheng He",r:["china","explor","giraffe"],
p:[["China’s great admiral","Zheng He led seven huge voyages for the Ming dynasty of China between 1405 and 1433."],["Treasure fleets","His fleets carried thousands of sailors on some of the largest wooden ships ever built."],["Across the Indian Ocean","His ships reached Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and the coast of East Africa."]],
l:"Zheng He was a Chinese admiral and diplomat who commanded seven great voyages across the Indian Ocean in the early 1400s, decades before European ships reached Asia by sea. His treasure fleets, with hundreds of ships and tens of thousands of men, carried gifts, traded goods and spread the fame of the Ming emperor from Southeast Asia to Africa.",
s:[["Early life","Zheng He was born around 1371 in Yunnan, in southwest China, into a Muslim family named Ma. When Ming armies conquered Yunnan, he was captured as a boy and taken to serve in the household of a prince, Zhu Di. He became a trusted officer and was given the name Zheng."],["The emperor’s envoy","When Zhu Di became the Yongle Emperor in 1402, he chose Zheng He to lead great fleets overseas. The voyages were meant to show China’s power and wealth, bring foreign rulers to pay respect to the emperor, and encourage trade. The first fleet sailed in 1405."],["Seven voyages","Between 1405 and 1433, Zheng He led seven voyages. His fleets visited Champa in Vietnam, Java, Sumatra, Malacca, Sri Lanka, Calicut in India, Hormuz in Persia, Arabia and the East African coast, including Mogadishu and Malindi. Each voyage lasted about two years."],["Treasure ships","The largest treasure ships are described in Chinese records as more than 120 meters long, though many historians think they were smaller. The first fleet is said to have had more than 300 ships and about 27,000 men, including sailors, soldiers, doctors, translators and astronomers."]],
k:[["Born","About 1371, Yunnan, China"],["Died","1433 or 1435, probably at sea or in Calicut"],["Nationality","Chinese (Ming dynasty)"],["Known for","Seven voyages across the Indian Ocean"],["Voyages","1405–1433"]],
d:[["c. 1371","Born Ma He in Yunnan"],["1405","First voyage sets sail"],["1414","A giraffe from Malindi is brought back to the Ming court"],["1431","Seventh and last voyage begins"],["2005","China celebrates the 600th anniversary of the first voyage"]],
s2:[["Gifts and trade","The fleets carried silk, porcelain, lacquer and gold to give to foreign rulers and to trade. They brought back spices, gems, ivory, medicines and exotic animals, including ostriches, zebras and giraffes. Ambassadors from dozens of kingdoms traveled to China on Zheng He’s ships."],["The giraffe","In 1414 one of Zheng He’s fleets brought back a giraffe that had been sent from Malindi in East Africa. At court it was said to be a qilin, a magical creature that appears in times of wise rule. Artists painted it, and it became one of the most famous gifts of the voyages."],["Southeast Asia","Zheng He’s fleets stopped often in Southeast Asian ports such as Malacca, which became an ally of China and a great trading city. Temples in Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand honor him, and some Chinese communities in the region still celebrate his memory."],["The voyages end","After the Yongle Emperor died in 1424, officials argued that the voyages cost too much. After the seventh voyage, China stopped sending great fleets and turned its attention to defending its northern borders. Many records of the voyages were later lost or destroyed."],["Envoys to China","Zheng He’s voyages brought rulers from distant lands to the Ming court. The king of Brunei died during a visit to Nanjing in 1408, and his tomb is still there. Parameswara, the founder of Malacca, visited in 1411, and in 1417 the sultan of Sulu, Paduka Batara, traveled to China and died there; his tomb in Dezhou, Shandong, is still cared for by his descendants."],["Remembering Zheng He","Zheng He is remembered as a great navigator and a symbol of peaceful contact between China and the world. In 2005 China celebrated the 600th anniversary of his first voyage and made 11 July its National Maritime Day."]],
sp:["A sultan of Sulu in China","In 1417 Paduka Batara, sultan of Sulu, sailed to China with about 340 people to meet the Yongle Emperor. On the journey home he fell ill and died in Dezhou. The emperor ordered a royal tomb built, and some of the sultan’s family stayed in China. Their descendants still live there today."],
g:[["Admiral","A commander of a fleet of ships"],["Envoy","A messenger sent by a ruler"],["Dynasty","A line of rulers from the same family"],["Porcelain","Fine, hard pottery first made in China"]],
f:["Zheng He’s first fleet may have had more than 300 ships.","One of his fleets brought a giraffe to China in 1414.","His voyages began 87 years before Columbus crossed the Atlantic."],
fun:"Zheng He’s fleets sailed to East Africa decades before Europeans reached Asia by sea."});

ADDART({id:"ibnbattuta",t:"Ibn Battuta",c:"People",i:"🧭",w:"Ibn Battuta",r:["islam","marcopolo","silk"],
p:[["The great traveler","Ibn Battuta was a Moroccan scholar who traveled for nearly 30 years in the 1300s."],["Across three continents","He visited lands from West Africa to China, covering about 120,000 kilometers."],["His book","His travels were written down in a famous book called the Rihla, meaning The Journey."]],
l:"Ibn Battuta was a Muslim scholar from Morocco who became one of the greatest travelers in history. Setting out on a pilgrimage to Mecca in 1325, he kept going for almost three decades, journeying through Africa, the Middle East, Central Asia, India, Southeast Asia and China. His book, the Rihla, is a priceless record of the 14th-century world.",
s:[["Setting out","Ibn Battuta was born in 1304 in Tangier, Morocco, into a family of judges and scholars. At 21, he left home to make the hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca. He later wrote that he set out alone, leaving his parents as birds leave their nests. He would not see Morocco again for 24 years."],["The Islamic world","In the 1300s, the Islamic world stretched from Spain and West Africa to India and Southeast Asia. Ibn Battuta could travel widely because Muslim rulers welcomed scholars, Arabic was widely understood, and Islamic law was used across many lands. He often worked as a judge to earn his keep."],["India and the Maldives","Ibn Battuta spent about eight years in Delhi serving as a judge for the sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq, a generous but dangerous ruler. Sent as an envoy to China, he was shipwrecked, then spent time in the Maldives, where he again served as a judge and married into local families."],["China and home","He eventually reached China, describing its paper money, porcelain and great port cities, and returned home in 1349. He then traveled to Spain and across the Sahara to the Mali Empire. In 1354 the sultan of Morocco ordered his travels written down by the scholar Ibn Juzayy."]],
k:[["Born","24 February 1304, Tangier, Morocco"],["Died","About 1368–1369, Morocco"],["Nationality","Moroccan"],["Known for","Travels across Africa and Asia"],["Distance traveled","About 120,000 km"]],
d:[["1304","Born in Tangier"],["1325","Leaves home on pilgrimage to Mecca"],["1334","Arrives in Delhi and serves the sultan as a judge"],["1352","Crosses the Sahara to the Mali Empire"],["1355","His Rihla is completed"]],
s2:[["How far he went","Ibn Battuta’s journeys covered about 120,000 kilometers, more than Marco Polo and almost any traveler before the age of steam. He visited the lands of about 40 present-day countries, traveling by camel caravan, horse, river boat and ocean ship."],["Dangers on the road","Ibn Battuta faced bandits, storms, shipwreck and illness. He was robbed and nearly killed in India and lived through the Black Death in the Middle East in 1348. Many of his companions and possessions were lost along the way."],["The Mali Empire","In 1352 and 1353 he crossed the Sahara to the Mali Empire, the land made famous by Mansa Musa. He described its safety for travelers, its justice and the city of Timbuktu, though he also criticized some local customs. His account is one of the few written descriptions of Mali at its height."],["Southeast Asia","On his way to China, Ibn Battuta stopped in Sumatra, at the Muslim kingdom of Samudra Pasai, one of the earliest in Southeast Asia. He also described a land he called Tawalisi, ruled by a warrior princess. Some writers have suggested it was in the Philippines, but historians do not agree where it was."],["The Rihla","The Rihla is a lively account full of rulers, customs, foods and adventures. Because Ibn Battuta dictated it years later from memory, some parts may be mixed up or borrowed from other writers. Still, it remains one of the most important sources on the medieval Islamic world."],["Remembered today","Ibn Battuta’s name lives on in airports, streets and a shopping mall in Dubai designed around the lands he visited. A crater on the Moon is named after him. He is one of the most famous figures in Moroccan history."]],
sp:["The traveler who kept going","Ibn Battuta set out only to visit Mecca, but in Alexandria a holy man told him he would travel to India and China. He took it as a sign. After the pilgrimage he made a rule never to travel the same road twice if he could avoid it, and he kept exploring for the rest of his travels."],
g:[["Hajj","The pilgrimage to Mecca that Muslims try to make once in their lives"],["Rihla","A book describing a journey"],["Caravan","A group of travelers, often with camels, crossing a desert"],["Sultan","A Muslim ruler"]],
f:["Ibn Battuta traveled about 120,000 kilometers.","He was away from Morocco for 24 years.","A crater on the Moon is named after him."],
fun:"Ibn Battuta traveled about three times as far as Marco Polo."});

ADDART({id:"columbus",t:"Christopher Columbus",c:"People",i:"🧭",w:"Christopher Columbus",r:["explor","magellan","aztec"],
p:[["Across the Atlantic","Christopher Columbus was an Italian sailor who crossed the Atlantic Ocean for Spain in 1492."],["Looking for Asia","He hoped to reach Asia by sailing west and believed until his death that he had."],["Two worlds meet","His voyages began lasting contact between Europe and the Americas, with huge effects on both."]],
l:"Christopher Columbus was a sailor from Genoa, in present-day Italy, whose voyages for the Spanish crown opened lasting contact between Europe and the Americas. In 1492 he sailed west across the Atlantic, hoping to reach Asia, and landed in the Caribbean. His four voyages changed world history, though they also brought conquest, disease and suffering to the peoples of the Americas.",
s:[["Early life","Columbus was born in Genoa in 1451. He went to sea as a young man and later settled in Portugal, a center of ocean exploration. He studied maps and believed that Asia could be reached by sailing west. He thought Earth was much smaller than it really is, and he did not know the Americas lay in the way."],["Finding a sponsor","Portugal turned down his plan. After years of asking, Columbus won the support of Queen Isabella and King Ferdinand of Spain in 1492, the same year their armies captured Granada, the last Muslim kingdom in Spain."],["The first voyage","Columbus sailed from Palos, Spain, on 3 August 1492 with three ships, the Niña, the Pinta and the Santa María, and about 90 men. On 12 October a lookout sighted land, an island in the Bahamas that its people, the Taíno, called Guanahani. He went on to explore Cuba and Hispaniola."],["Four voyages","Columbus made four voyages between 1492 and 1504, exploring Caribbean islands and the coasts of Central America and Venezuela. As governor of Hispaniola he ruled harshly, and in 1500 he was arrested and sent back to Spain in chains. He was freed but lost his governorship. He died in Spain in 1506."]],
k:[["Born","1451, Genoa"],["Died","20 May 1506, Valladolid, Spain"],["Nationality","Genoese (Italian), sailing for Spain"],["Known for","Voyages across the Atlantic to the Americas"],["Voyages","4, from 1492 to 1504"]],
d:[["1451","Born in Genoa"],["1492","Lands in the Bahamas on 12 October"],["1493","Second voyage brings settlers and animals to Hispaniola"],["1500","Arrested and sent back to Spain in chains"],["1506","Dies in Valladolid, Spain"]],
s2:[["Not the first","Columbus was not the first person to reach the Americas. Its Indigenous peoples had lived there for at least 15,000 years, and Norse sailors led by Leif Erikson reached Newfoundland around the year 1000. But Columbus’s voyages, unlike the Norse visit, led to lasting contact between the hemispheres."],["The Taíno","The Taíno people of the Caribbean welcomed Columbus at first. Within a few decades, war, forced labor and diseases brought from Europe, such as smallpox, to which they had no immunity, had killed most of them. Many Caribbean people today still have some Taíno ancestry."],["The Columbian Exchange","After Columbus, plants, animals and diseases crossed the Atlantic in both directions, an event called the Columbian Exchange. Europe, Africa and Asia gained maize, potatoes, tomatoes, cacao and chili peppers, while the Americas gained horses, cattle, wheat and sugar cane, along with deadly diseases."],["Crops around the world","Spain’s empire linked the Americas to Asia as well as Europe. From 1565 Spanish galleons sailed between Acapulco in Mexico and Manila, carrying silver west and silk and spices east. American crops spread along these routes: chili peppers became central to the cooking of India, Thailand, Korea and Sichuan, sweet potatoes and maize helped feed China, and cassava became a staple in Africa."],["A disputed hero","Columbus was long celebrated as a hero of discovery, and many places are named after him, including Colombia. Today many people also remember the suffering his arrival brought to Indigenous peoples, and some places have replaced Columbus Day with Indigenous Peoples’ Day."],["Why America is not called Columbia","The Americas are named after another Italian explorer, Amerigo Vespucci, who argued that the lands were a New World, not part of Asia. In 1507 a German mapmaker, Martin Waldseemüller, wrote America on his world map, and the name stuck."]],
sp:["A small world","Columbus believed Earth was much smaller than it is and that Asia stretched farther east. He calculated that Japan lay about 4,000 kilometers west of the Canary Islands; the real distance is about 19,000 kilometers. Had the Americas not been in the way, his ships would have run out of food and water long before reaching Asia."],
g:[["Voyage","A long journey by sea"],["Columbian Exchange","The transfer of plants, animals and diseases between the Americas and the rest of the world"],["Indigenous","Belonging to the original people of a place"],["Immunity","The body’s ability to resist a disease"]],
f:["Columbus believed until his death that he had reached Asia.","The Americas are named after Amerigo Vespucci.","Chili peppers from the Americas became central to cooking in India, Thailand and Korea."],
fun:"Columbus thought Japan was about 4,000 kilometers west of the Canary Islands; it is about 19,000."});

;
/* data/more3.js */
// Articles for thin sub-sections, part 3: Matter and Chemistry (states of matter, chemical reactions, plastics).
ADDART({id:"states",t:"States of Matter",c:"Science",i:"🧊",w:"State of matter",r:["atom","wcycle","acid"],
p:[["Solid, liquid, gas","Everything around us is matter, and on Earth it is usually found as a solid, a liquid or a gas."],["Changing state","Heating or cooling can change matter from one state to another, as when ice melts or water boils."],["The fourth state","Plasma, a glowing gas of charged particles, is the most common state of visible matter in the universe."]],
l:"Matter is anything that has mass and takes up space, and it can exist in different states. On Earth the most familiar are solid, liquid and gas, but most of the visible matter in the universe is plasma, the glowing material of stars. What state a substance is in depends on how its particles move, and that depends on temperature and pressure.",
s:[["Particles in motion","All matter is made of tiny particles, atoms and molecules, that are always moving. In a solid they are packed tightly and only vibrate in place. In a liquid they are close together but can slide past one another. In a gas they are far apart and fly around freely, filling any container."],["Solids","Solids keep their own shape and volume. In crystals such as salt, ice and diamond, the particles are arranged in a regular repeating pattern. Other solids, such as glass, have particles arranged in a jumble. Solids can be hard or soft, brittle or bendy."],["Liquids","Liquids keep their volume but take the shape of their container. They flow and can be poured. Some, such as honey, flow slowly because their particles cling together, a property called viscosity. The surface of a liquid acts like a thin skin, which lets water striders walk on ponds."],["Gases","Gases have no fixed shape or volume. They spread out to fill any space and can be squeezed into smaller ones, which is why air can be pumped into tires and divers can carry it in tanks. Heating a gas makes its particles move faster, so it pushes harder on its container."]],
k:[["Common states","Solid, liquid, gas, plasma"],["Water melts","0 °C"],["Water boils","100 °C at sea level"],["Dry ice changes to gas","About −78 °C"],["Most common in the universe","Plasma"]],
d:[["1662","Robert Boyle publishes his law linking the pressure and volume of a gas"],["1787","Jacques Charles finds that gases expand when heated"],["1928","Irving Langmuir names plasma"],["1995","Scientists create a Bose–Einstein condensate, a new state of matter"],["2001","Nobel Prize in Physics awarded for Bose–Einstein condensates"]],
s2:[["Changing state","Melting turns a solid into a liquid, and freezing turns it back. Evaporation and boiling turn a liquid into a gas, and condensation turns a gas into a liquid, as when water droplets form on a cold glass. Some solids, such as dry ice, turn straight into gas, which is called sublimation."],["Water is unusual","Most substances shrink when they freeze, but water expands, so ice is lighter than liquid water and floats. This keeps lakes from freezing solid, letting fish survive under the ice. It also means freezing water can burst pipes and split rocks, slowly breaking mountains apart."],["Pressure matters","The boiling point of water depends on air pressure. On a high mountain, where the pressure is lower, water boils at a lower temperature, so food takes longer to cook. A pressure cooker raises the pressure, so water boils hotter and food cooks faster."],["Plasma","When a gas gets extremely hot, electrons are stripped from its atoms, making plasma, a mix of charged particles. Stars, including the Sun, are made of plasma. On Earth it appears in lightning, auroras, neon signs and some televisions. Plasma conducts electricity and responds to magnets."],["Strange states","Near absolute zero, about −273 °C, some atoms merge into a single super-atom called a Bose–Einstein condensate, first made in a laboratory in 1995. Other unusual materials include liquid crystals, used in phone and computer screens, and supercritical fluids, which are partly like liquids and partly like gases."],["States in everyday life","Cooking, weather and machines all depend on changes of state. Evaporating sweat cools the body. Refrigerators work by evaporating and condensing a special fluid. Clouds form when water vapor condenses into droplets, and rain falls when the droplets grow heavy, part of the water cycle."]],
sp:["Why ice floats","When water freezes, its molecules line up in an open, six-sided pattern held together by hydrogen bonds, leaving more space between them than in liquid water. That makes ice about 9 percent less dense than water. The same six-sided pattern is why snowflakes have six arms."],
g:[["Matter","Anything that has mass and takes up space"],["Evaporation","A liquid changing into a gas"],["Condensation","A gas changing into a liquid"],["Sublimation","A solid changing straight into a gas"]],
f:["Ice floats because water expands when it freezes.","Water boils at a lower temperature on high mountains.","Stars are made of plasma, the fourth state of matter."],
fun:"Most of the visible matter in the universe is plasma, the state of matter in stars."});

ADDART({id:"reactions",t:"Chemical Reactions",c:"Science",i:"⚗️",w:"Chemical reaction",r:["atom","acid","alchemy"],
p:[["Making new substances","In a chemical reaction, substances change into new substances with different properties."],["Everywhere around us","Cooking, rusting, burning and digestion are all chemical reactions."],["Nothing is lost","Atoms are rearranged in a reaction but never created or destroyed."]],
l:"A chemical reaction is a process in which substances, called reactants, change into new substances, called products. Bonds between atoms break and new bonds form. Chemical reactions happen all the time, in our bodies, in kitchens, in factories and in nature, and chemists use them to make medicines, fuels, fertilizers and materials of every kind.",
s:[["Signs of a reaction","You can often tell a chemical reaction is happening when a gas bubbles out, the color changes, light or heat is given off, a solid forms in a liquid, or a new smell appears. Baking soda fizzing in vinegar, a match bursting into flame and an apple browning are all reactions."],["Physical or chemical?","A physical change, such as melting ice or dissolving sugar, does not make a new substance and is often easy to reverse. A chemical change makes new substances and is usually hard to undo. You cannot easily turn a cooked egg or burned paper back into what it was."],["Equations","Chemists write reactions as equations, with the reactants on the left and the products on the right. For example, hydrogen burns in oxygen to make water: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. The equation is balanced, with the same number of each kind of atom on both sides."],["Conservation of mass","In the 1770s and 1780s, the French chemist Antoine Lavoisier showed by careful weighing that the total mass of substances stays the same during a reaction. Atoms are only rearranged. His work helped turn chemistry into a modern science and ended old ideas about burning."]],
k:[["Reactants","Substances that react"],["Products","New substances made"],["Law","Mass is conserved"],["Catalyst","Speeds up a reaction without being used up"],["Example","2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"]],
d:[["1789","Lavoisier publishes his textbook stating the conservation of mass"],["1808","John Dalton explains reactions with his atomic theory"],["1835","Jöns Jacob Berzelius names catalysis"],["1909","Fritz Haber shows how to make ammonia from the air"],["1912","Louis-Camille Maillard describes the browning reaction in cooking"]],
s2:[["Burning","Burning, or combustion, is a fast reaction between a fuel and oxygen that gives off heat and light. Burning wood, gas or gasoline makes carbon dioxide and water. A fire needs fuel, oxygen and heat; taking away any one of these, the fire triangle, puts it out."],["Rusting","Iron slowly reacts with oxygen and water to form rust, a flaky reddish substance. Rust weakens bridges, cars and ships, especially near salty sea air. Paint, oil, galvanizing with zinc and stainless steel all help protect iron from rusting."],["Hot and cold reactions","Some reactions give out heat; these are exothermic, like burning and the hand warmers that heat up when opened. Others take in heat and feel cold; these are endothermic, like the instant cold packs used for sports injuries. Photosynthesis is an endothermic reaction powered by sunlight."],["Speeding up reactions","Reactions go faster when substances are hotter, more concentrated or broken into smaller pieces. Catalysts speed up reactions without being used up. The catalytic converter in a car turns harmful exhaust gases into less harmful ones, and enzymes are catalysts in living things."],["Feeding the world","In 1909 Fritz Haber found how to combine nitrogen from the air with hydrogen to make ammonia, and Carl Bosch scaled it up for factories. Ammonia is used to make fertilizer, which helps grow food for billions of people. The same chemistry was also used to make explosives in World War I."],["Chemistry in the kitchen","Cooking is full of reactions. Browning bread, grilling meat and roasting coffee involve the Maillard reaction between sugars and proteins, which creates hundreds of flavors. Baking powder releases carbon dioxide to make cakes rise, and yeast ferments sugar to make bread fluffy."]],
sp:["The father of modern chemistry","Antoine Lavoisier named oxygen and hydrogen, showed that burning is a reaction with oxygen and wrote one of the first modern chemistry textbooks in 1789. He also worked as a tax collector for the French king, and in 1794, during the French Revolution, he was executed. A fellow scientist said it took a moment to cut off his head, but France might not produce another like it in a hundred years."],
g:[["Reactant","A substance that takes part in a reaction"],["Product","A substance made by a reaction"],["Catalyst","Something that speeds up a reaction without being used up"],["Exothermic","Giving out heat"]],
f:["Atoms are never created or destroyed in a chemical reaction.","Rusting is a slow chemical reaction.","Ammonia made from air helps feed billions of people."],
fun:"The browning of toast, grilled meat and roasted coffee is the same chemical reaction."});

ADDART({id:"plastics",t:"Plastics and Polymers",c:"Science",i:"🧴",w:"Plastic",r:["reactions","pacific","seaturtle","polymers"],
p:[["Long-chain molecules","Plastics are made of polymers, very long molecules built from many small repeating units."],["Useful everywhere","Light, cheap and easy to shape, plastics are used for packaging, clothes, cars, toys and medical equipment."],["A pollution problem","Plastic lasts for hundreds of years, and huge amounts end up in rivers and oceans."]],
l:"Plastics are materials made of polymers, giant molecules built from thousands of small repeating units. Most are made from oil and natural gas. Since the mid-1900s, plastics have transformed everyday life, but their long life has also made plastic waste one of the world’s biggest pollution problems, especially in the oceans.",
s:[["What is a polymer?","A polymer is a long chain made by linking many small molecules, called monomers, like beads on a necklace. Nature makes polymers too: cellulose in wood, starch in rice, silk, rubber and the DNA in our cells are all polymers. Plastics are synthetic polymers made in factories."],["Kinds of plastic","Thermoplastics, such as polyethylene in plastic bags and PET in drink bottles, soften when heated and can be reshaped and recycled. Thermosets, such as the melamine used in some plates, set hard for good when made and cannot be melted again. Recycling codes from 1 to 7 on packaging show the type of plastic."],["Why plastics are useful","Plastics are light, strong, waterproof and cheap, and can be molded into almost any shape. They keep food fresh, insulate electric wires, make cars lighter and save fuel, and are used for syringes, gloves and other medical equipment that must be clean and used only once."],["How long plastic lasts","Most plastics do not rot. Instead they break into smaller and smaller pieces. Pieces smaller than 5 millimeters are called microplastics, and they have been found in the deepest oceans, in Arctic ice, in drinking water and even in human blood. A plastic bottle may last hundreds of years."]],
k:[["Made from","Mostly oil and natural gas"],["World production","About 400 million metric tons a year"],["Ever recycled","About 9 percent"],["Microplastics","Pieces under 5 mm"],["First plastic","Parkesine, 1862"]],
d:[["1862","Alexander Parkes shows Parkesine, the first plastic, in London"],["1907","Leo Baekeland invents Bakelite, the first fully synthetic plastic"],["1935","Wallace Carothers invents nylon"],["2008","Rwanda bans plastic bags"],["2022","UN members agree to negotiate a global treaty on plastic pollution"]],
s2:[["The plastic age","Bakelite, made in 1907, was used for telephones, radios and electrical plugs. Nylon, invented in 1935, replaced silk in stockings and parachutes. After World War II, cheap plastics such as polyethylene and PVC spread into every home, and production has grown from about 2 million metric tons in 1950 to about 400 million metric tons a year."],["Plastic in the ocean","Millions of metric tons of plastic reach the oceans each year, mostly carried by rivers. Sea turtles mistake bags for jellyfish, seabirds feed bottle caps to their chicks and whales have been found with stomachs full of plastic. Floating plastic gathers in huge areas such as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch."],["Single-use packaging","Much plastic waste comes from packaging used once and thrown away, such as bottles, bags and the small sachets in which shampoo, coffee and sauces are sold in many countries. A 2021 study found that about 1,000 rivers carry most of the plastic that reaches the sea, and that many of the largest sources are in Asia, especially in the Philippines, India and Malaysia, where waste collection has not kept up with use."],["Reduce, reuse, recycle","The best way to cut plastic waste is to use less of it, using reusable bags, bottles and containers. Recycling turns used plastic into new products, but only some kinds can be recycled easily, and only about 9 percent of all plastic ever made has been recycled. Many cities have banned single-use bags and straws."],["Laws and solutions","Many countries have passed laws against plastic waste. Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008 and Kenya in 2017, the European Union banned many single-use plastic items from 2021, and some countries make companies responsible for collecting the packaging they sell. Countries are also negotiating a global treaty on plastic pollution."],["New materials","Scientists are developing bioplastics made from plants such as corn and cassava, plastics that break down more easily, and ways to recycle plastics chemically back into their building blocks. In 2016 Japanese scientists discovered bacteria that can eat PET plastic."]],
sp:["Plastic-eating bacteria","In 2016 scientists in Japan found bacteria at a bottle recycling site that had evolved to digest PET, the plastic used in drink bottles. The bacteria use two enzymes to break the plastic into its building blocks. Researchers have since improved the enzymes in the hope of recycling plastic bottles back into new ones."],
g:[["Polymer","A very long molecule made of repeating units"],["Monomer","A small molecule that links up to make a polymer"],["Microplastic","A piece of plastic smaller than 5 millimeters"],["Single-use","Made to be used once and thrown away"]],
f:["Only about 9 percent of all plastic ever made has been recycled.","DNA, silk and rubber are natural polymers.","Microplastics have been found in Arctic ice and the deepest oceans."],
fun:"Silk, rubber, starch and DNA are all natural polymers, like plastics."});

;
/* data/more4.js */
// Articles for thin sub-sections, part 4: Exploring Space (rockets, the James Webb Space Telescope)
// and Plants and Habitats (flowers, fungi).
ADDART({id:"rocket",t:"Rockets",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🚀",w:"Rocket",r:["space","iss","sat","momentum"],
p:[["Pushing against nothing","A rocket flies by pushing hot gas out of its back, which pushes the rocket forward."],["The way to space","Rockets are the only machines that can lift people and satellites into space."],["From fireworks to the Moon","Rockets began as Chinese fireworks and weapons and grew into giants that carried astronauts to the Moon."]],
l:"A rocket is a vehicle that moves by blasting hot gas out of its engine at high speed. Because it carries its own fuel and oxygen, it can work in the airless vacuum of space. Rockets launch satellites, space probes and astronauts, and they have taken humans to the Moon and robots to every planet in the Solar System.",
s:[["How rockets work","A rocket works by Newton’s third law of motion: every action has an equal and opposite reaction. When the engine forces gas out of the nozzle at great speed, the gas pushes back on the rocket and drives it forward. You can see the same effect by letting go of a blown-up balloon."],["Fuel and oxidizer","Fires need oxygen, but there is no air in space, so rockets carry an oxidizer as well as fuel. Some rockets burn liquid fuels, such as kerosene or liquid hydrogen, with liquid oxygen. Others use solid fuel packed into the rocket, like a giant firework."],["Stages","Most space rockets are built in stages stacked on top of each other. When a stage runs out of fuel, it drops away, so the rocket no longer has to carry its empty weight. The upper stages, now much lighter, can reach much higher speeds."],["Reaching orbit","To stay in orbit around Earth, a spacecraft must travel sideways at about 28,000 kilometers per hour, fast enough to keep falling around the planet without hitting it. To escape Earth’s gravity completely, it must reach about 40,000 kilometers per hour."]],
k:[["Principle","Newton’s third law"],["Orbital speed","About 28,000 km/h"],["Escape speed","About 40,000 km/h"],["Tallest flown","Starship, about 120 m with its booster"],["First liquid-fuel rocket","1926, Robert Goddard"]],
d:[["1232","Chinese armies use fire arrows, early rockets, at the siege of Kaifeng"],["1926","Robert Goddard launches the first liquid-fuel rocket"],["1957","A Soviet rocket launches Sputnik 1, the first satellite"],["1969","A Saturn V rocket carries Apollo 11 to the Moon"],["2015","A Falcon 9 booster makes the first landing of an orbital rocket"]],
s2:[["Early rockets","The Chinese used gunpowder rockets as fireworks and weapons by the 1200s. In the 1700s, the armies of Mysore in India used iron-cased rockets against the British, who copied them. The rockets red glare in the US national anthem refers to these British war rockets."],["Rocket pioneers","Konstantin Tsiolkovsky of Russia worked out the mathematics of spaceflight in 1903. Robert Goddard of the United States launched the first liquid-fuel rocket in 1926; it flew for just 2.5 seconds. Hermann Oberth in Germany inspired a generation of rocket builders."],["War and the space race","In World War II, Germany built the V-2, the first large guided rocket, using forced labor. After the war, its engineers worked for the United States and the Soviet Union. The rivalry of the Cold War space race led to the first satellite in 1957 and the first human in space in 1961."],["Saturn V","The Saturn V, which carried astronauts to the Moon from 1969 to 1972, was about 111 meters tall and weighed nearly 3,000 metric tons when fueled. Its first stage burned about 13 metric tons of fuel and oxygen every second. It remains one of the most powerful machines ever flown."],["Reusable rockets","For decades, almost all rockets were thrown away after one flight. In 2015 the company SpaceX landed a Falcon 9 first stage upright after launch, and reused boosters now fly again and again, greatly cutting costs. Larger reusable rockets, such as Starship, are being tested."],["Spaceports of the world","Rockets are launched from spaceports, usually near the coast so that rockets fly over the sea. Major sites include Baikonur in Kazakhstan, Cape Canaveral in Florida, Kourou in French Guiana, close to the equator, Sriharikota in India, Jiuquan and Wenchang in China and Tanegashima in Japan. Private companies now run launch sites in the United States and New Zealand."]],
sp:["The balloon rocket","You can make a rocket at home. Thread a string through a straw and tie the string tight across a room. Blow up a balloon, hold its neck closed and tape it to the straw. When you let go, air rushes out of the balloon and pushes it along the string, showing Newton’s third law in action."],
g:[["Thrust","The pushing force produced by a rocket engine"],["Oxidizer","A substance that supplies oxygen for burning"],["Stage","A section of a rocket with its own engines and fuel"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"]],
f:["Rockets carry their own oxygen because there is no air in space.","Goddard’s first liquid-fuel rocket flew for 2.5 seconds.","The Saturn V was about 111 meters tall."],
fun:"Robert Goddard’s first liquid-fuel rocket in 1926 flew for just 2.5 seconds."});

ADDART({id:"jwst",t:"The James Webb Space Telescope",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔭",w:"James Webb Space Telescope",r:["scope","hubble","bigbang","earlygalaxies","exodetect","hotjupiters","orionnebula","whirlpool"],
p:[["The largest space telescope","The James Webb Space Telescope is the largest and most powerful telescope ever sent into space."],["Seeing heat","It sees mainly infrared light, letting it look through dust and back to the early universe."],["A million miles away","It orbits the Sun about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth."]],
l:"The James Webb Space Telescope, often called JWST or Webb, is the largest and most powerful space telescope ever built. Launched on Christmas Day 2021, it studies the universe in infrared light, seeing the first galaxies that formed after the Big Bang, stars being born inside dusty clouds and the atmospheres of planets around other stars.",
s:[["A golden mirror","Webb’s main mirror is 6.5 meters across, nearly three times wider than Hubble’s. It is made of 18 six-sided segments of beryllium coated with a thin layer of gold, which reflects infrared light very well. The mirror was folded to fit inside the rocket and unfolded in space."],["The sunshield","Infrared telescopes must be very cold, or their own heat would blind them. Webb has a five-layer sunshield about the size of a tennis court that blocks heat from the Sun, Earth and Moon. The side facing the Sun can reach about 85 °C, while the telescope side stays at about −233 °C."],["Far from Earth","Webb orbits a point in space called L2, about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, on the side away from the Sun. There the Sun, Earth and Moon are always behind the sunshield. It is too far away for astronauts to repair, so everything had to work perfectly."],["Infrared eyes","Light from very distant galaxies is stretched to longer, redder wavelengths as the universe expands. Webb’s infrared cameras can catch this stretched light from galaxies that formed only a few hundred million years after the Big Bang. Infrared also passes through clouds of dust that hide newborn stars."]],
k:[["Launched","25 December 2021"],["Mirror","6.5 m, 18 gold-coated segments"],["Distance from Earth","About 1.5 million km"],["Operating temperature","About −233 °C"],["Partners","NASA, ESA and the Canadian Space Agency"]],
d:[["1990","The Hubble Space Telescope is launched"],["1996","Planning begins for a next-generation space telescope"],["2021","Webb launches on an Ariane 5 rocket from French Guiana"],["2022","The first full-color images are released on 12 July"],["2022","Webb detects carbon dioxide in the atmosphere of a distant planet"]],
s2:[["Launch and unfolding","Webb launched on 25 December 2021 aboard a European Ariane 5 rocket from Kourou, French Guiana. Over the next weeks it unfolded its sunshield and mirror in hundreds of steps, each of which had to work. Engineers called it 29 days on the edge. It reached its orbit in January 2022."],["First images","On 12 July 2022 NASA released Webb’s first full-color images, including a deep field crowded with thousands of galaxies, the Carina Nebula’s cosmic cliffs of gas and dust, and the Southern Ring Nebula around a dying star. Its pictures have amazed scientists and the public."],["The early universe","Webb has found galaxies that existed when the universe was only about 300 million years old, earlier and brighter than many scientists expected. These discoveries are helping astronomers understand how the first stars and galaxies formed after the Big Bang."],["Other worlds","By studying starlight passing through the atmospheres of exoplanets, Webb can detect gases such as water vapor, carbon dioxide and methane. In 2022 it made the first clear detection of carbon dioxide in an exoplanet’s atmosphere, on a giant planet called WASP-39b."],["Who was James Webb?","The telescope is named after James E. Webb, who led NASA from 1961 to 1968 and oversaw the build-up to the Apollo Moon landings. The choice of name was debated, but NASA kept it after an investigation. Webb was planned as the successor to the Hubble Space Telescope."],["Webb and Hubble","Hubble, launched in 1990, sees mostly visible and ultraviolet light from low Earth orbit, where astronauts have repaired it five times. Webb sees infrared light from much farther away. The two telescopes often work together, studying the same objects in different kinds of light."]],
sp:["Six-sided mirrors","Webb’s mirror is made of 18 hexagons because six-sided shapes fit together without gaps and form a nearly circular mirror. Each segment can be moved by tiny motors in steps smaller than one ten-thousandth of a hair’s width, so all 18 act as one perfect mirror. About 48 grams of gold coats the whole mirror."],
g:[["Infrared","Light with longer wavelengths than we can see, felt as heat"],["Exoplanet","A planet orbiting a star other than the Sun"],["Deep field","A long-exposure image of a small patch of sky full of distant galaxies"],["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"]],
f:["Webb’s mirror is coated with about 48 grams of gold.","Its sunshield is about the size of a tennis court.","Webb launched on Christmas Day 2021."],
fun:"The James Webb Space Telescope’s sunshield is about the size of a tennis court."});

ADDART({id:"flower",t:"Flowers and Pollination",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌺",w:"Flower",r:["bee","butterfly","photo","lotus","dandelions","alpineplants","plantevolution","wildflowers","saffron","lavender","corpseflower"],
p:[["How plants reproduce","A flower is the part of a plant that makes seeds."],["Helpers","Insects, birds, bats and the wind carry pollen from flower to flower."],["Most plants flower","Flowering plants make up about nine out of ten kinds of plant on Earth."]],
l:"Flowers are the reproductive parts of flowering plants, the largest group of plants on Earth. Their colors, scents and nectar attract animals that carry pollen from one flower to another, allowing seeds and fruits to form. Flowers feed many animals, give us most of our food crops and have been loved and given as gifts throughout history.",
s:[["Parts of a flower","Most flowers have green sepals that protect the bud and colorful petals that attract pollinators. The male parts, called stamens, make pollen in their anthers. The female part, the pistil, has a sticky stigma to catch pollen and an ovary at its base that holds the ovules, which become seeds."],["Pollination","Pollination happens when pollen moves from an anther to a stigma. A pollen grain then grows a tiny tube down to an ovule and fertilizes it. Some plants pollinate themselves, but most rely on wind or animals to carry pollen from other plants of the same kind, which keeps them healthier."],["Attracting pollinators","Flowers attract pollinators with color, scent, shape and rewards such as sweet nectar. Bees prefer blue, purple and yellow flowers and can see ultraviolet patterns that guide them to the nectar. Birds like red tubular flowers, and bats and moths visit pale, strong-smelling flowers at night."],["From flower to fruit","After fertilization, the petals fall, the ovules grow into seeds and the ovary often swells into a fruit that protects them. Mangoes, tomatoes, cucumbers and even rice grains develop from flowers. Fruits help spread seeds when animals eat them or when they float, blow or stick to fur."]],
k:[["Flowering plant species","About 300,000 or more"],["Largest single flower","Rafflesia arnoldii, up to about 1 m"],["Earliest fossils","About 130 million years old"],["Largest flower exporter","The Netherlands"],["Main pollinators","Bees, butterflies, moths, birds, bats, wind"]],
d:[["c. 130 million years ago","The first flowering plants appear"],["1694","Rudolf Camerarius shows that plants reproduce sexually"],["1793","Christian Sprengel explains how insects pollinate flowers"],["1753","Linnaeus names thousands of plants in Species Plantarum"],["1818","Europeans first record Rafflesia arnoldii, the largest flower, in Sumatra"]],
s2:[["Wind-pollinated plants","Grasses, rice, corn and many trees are pollinated by wind. Their flowers are small and plain, without bright petals, scent or nectar, but they make huge amounts of light pollen. This pollen is what causes hay fever in many people."],["Rafflesia","Rafflesias are parasitic plants with no leaves, stems or roots of their own. They live inside jungle vines in the forests of Southeast Asia and burst out as huge, foul-smelling flowers that attract flies. Rafflesia arnoldii of Sumatra and Borneo has the largest single flower of any plant, up to about a meter across."],["Orchids","Orchids are one of the largest families of flowering plants, with about 28,000 species. Many trick insects into visiting by looking or smelling like a female insect or food. Orchids grow on every continent except Antarctica, and tropical countries such as Colombia, Ecuador, Indonesia and the Philippines have thousands of kinds."],["The sampaguita","Many countries choose a flower as a national symbol. The lotus stands for India, the rose for England and the United States, the tulip is linked with the Netherlands, the cherry blossom with Japan, the protea with South Africa and the sampaguita, a small, sweet-scented jasmine, with the Philippines. Such flowers appear on coins, stamps and badges."],["Pollinators in trouble","About three quarters of the world’s main food crops benefit from animal pollinators. Pollinators such as bees, butterflies and bats are declining because of habitat loss, pesticides, disease and climate change. Planting flowers and protecting wild habitats helps them survive."],["Flowers and people","People have grown flowers for beauty, scent and meaning for thousands of years. Ancient Egyptians placed flowers in tombs, and the tulip craze in the Netherlands in the 1630s made some bulbs cost more than houses. Today the Netherlands is the world’s largest flower exporter, and Colombia, Ecuador and Kenya grow millions of roses for sale abroad."]],
sp:["The bee’s-eye view","Many flowers have patterns that people cannot see but bees can, because bees see ultraviolet light. Under an ultraviolet camera, a plain yellow flower may show a dark bull’s-eye or lines leading to its center, like runway lights guiding a plane. Scientists call these nectar guides."],
g:[["Stamen","The male part of a flower that makes pollen"],["Pistil","The female part of a flower that holds the ovules"],["Pollination","Moving pollen from one flower to another"],["Nectar","A sweet liquid flowers make to attract animals"]],
f:["About nine out of ten kinds of plant are flowering plants.","Bees can see ultraviolet patterns on flowers.","Orchids make up roughly one in ten flowering plant species."],
fun:"Some flowers have ultraviolet patterns that guide bees like runway lights."});

ADDART({id:"fungi",t:"Fungi and Mushrooms",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🍄",w:"Fungus",r:["eco","penicil","trees","mycorrhiza","parasiticplants"],
p:[["Neither plant nor animal","Fungi, including mushrooms, molds and yeasts, form their own kingdom of life."],["Nature’s recyclers","Fungi break down dead plants and animals, returning nutrients to the soil."],["Food and medicine","Fungi give us mushrooms, bread, cheese, soy sauce and medicines such as penicillin."]],
l:"Fungi are living things that form a kingdom of their own, separate from plants and animals. They include mushrooms, molds, yeasts and mildews. Fungi cannot make their own food as plants do; instead they absorb it from their surroundings. As decomposers and partners of plants, they are essential to life on land, and they give people food, drinks and medicines.",
s:[["What is a fungus?","Fungi were once grouped with plants, but they have no chlorophyll and cannot photosynthesize. Their cell walls contain chitin, the same material as insect shells. Genetic studies show that fungi are actually more closely related to animals than to plants."],["Mycelium","The main body of most fungi is a hidden network of fine threads called hyphae, which together form the mycelium. It spreads through soil, wood or food, releasing enzymes that break it down and absorbing the nutrients. A mushroom is only the fruiting body, the part that makes spores."],["Spores","Fungi reproduce by spores, tiny cells released in enormous numbers. A single mushroom can release billions of spores, carried by wind, water and animals. If a spore lands somewhere damp with food, it grows into a new mycelium."],["Decomposers","Fungi and bacteria break down fallen leaves, dead wood and dead animals. Without them, forests would be buried under dead material and nutrients would not return to the soil. Some fungi can even break down tough wood fibers that few other living things can digest."]],
k:[["Described species","About 150,000"],["Estimated total","2 to 4 million or more"],["Largest organism","A honey fungus in Oregon, about 9 km²"],["Cell walls","Made of chitin"],["Closer relatives","Animals, not plants"]],
d:[["c. 420 million years ago","Prototaxites, a giant fungus up to 8 m tall, grows on land"],["1928","Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin made by a mold"],["1969","Robert Whittaker places fungi in their own kingdom"],["1997","Study shows forest trees share carbon through fungal networks"],["2003","A study maps a honey fungus in Oregon as one of the largest known organisms"]],
s2:[["Partners of plants","About nine out of ten land plants live in partnership with fungi called mycorrhizae, which wrap around or enter their roots. The fungi help the plant absorb water and minerals, and the plant gives the fungi sugar. Without these partners, many plants would grow poorly."],["Lichens","A lichen is a partnership between a fungus and an alga or bacterium that can make food from sunlight. Lichens grow on rocks, tree bark and walls in some of the harshest places on Earth, from deserts to Antarctica, and they slowly help break rock into soil."],["Mushrooms to eat","Many mushrooms are delicious and nutritious, such as button, shiitake and oyster mushrooms. They are central to many cuisines, from French truffles to Chinese and Japanese soups, and oyster and straw mushrooms are grown cheaply on rice straw and sawdust. Some wild mushrooms are deadly, so wild mushrooms should never be eaten unless an expert identifies them."],["Yeast and molds at work","Yeast is a single-celled fungus. It makes bread rise by releasing carbon dioxide and is used to brew drinks. Molds are used to make cheeses and to ferment soy sauce and miso. The medicine penicillin comes from a mold, and other fungi provide drugs used in organ transplants."],["Fungi that harm","Some fungi cause diseases in plants, animals and people. Rice blast fungus damages rice crops, and a fungus called chytrid has killed frogs around the world. In people, fungi cause athlete’s foot and ringworm. Molds can spoil food and grow on damp walls."],["The giant underground","A honey fungus in the Malheur National Forest in Oregon spreads underground across about 9 square kilometers, making it one of the largest living organisms known. Scientists estimate it may be thousands of years old. Above ground, it shows only as clusters of mushrooms in autumn."]],
sp:["The wood wide web","Under a forest floor, fungal threads link the roots of many trees. Studies have shown that carbon and nutrients can move between trees through these networks, which some call the wood wide web. Scientists still debate how much trees really help one another this way, but the networks themselves are real and vast."],
g:[["Mycelium","The network of threads that forms the main body of a fungus"],["Spore","A tiny cell that can grow into a new fungus"],["Decomposer","A living thing that breaks down dead material"],["Lichen","A partnership of a fungus and an alga or bacterium"]],
f:["Fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants.","A honey fungus in Oregon covers about 9 square kilometers.","Penicillin comes from a mold."],
fun:"Fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants."});

;
/* data/more5.js */
// Articles for thin sub-sections, part 5: Brain and Senses (smell, taste and touch; memory),
// Stage and Screen (Philippine cinema, television), Reptiles (lizards), Insects (ants) and Restless Earth (tsunamis).
ADDART({id:"smelltaste",t:"Smell, Taste and Touch",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👃",w:"Sense",r:["brain","skin","eye","touch"],
p:[["Three close senses","Smell, taste and touch tell us about the chemicals and objects around us."],["Flavor is mostly smell","Much of what we call taste actually comes from smell."],["Touch everywhere","The skin is packed with sensors for pressure, vibration, heat, cold and pain."]],
l:"Smell, taste and touch are three of the body’s senses, alongside sight and hearing. Smell and taste detect chemicals in the air and in food, warning us of danger and helping us enjoy what we eat. Touch, spread across the whole skin, tells us about pressure, texture, temperature and pain. Together they help keep us safe and connected to the world.",
s:[["Smell","High inside the nose is a small patch of tissue with millions of smell receptor cells. When we breathe in, odor molecules land on them, and signals travel straight to the brain’s smell center, which is closely linked to memory and emotion. That is why a scent can suddenly bring back an old memory."],["Taste","The tongue is covered in tiny bumps, many holding taste buds. They detect five basic tastes: sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami, the savory taste of foods such as broth, cheese and patis. Every part of the tongue can sense all five; the old tongue map showing separate zones is a myth."],["Flavor","Flavor combines taste, smell, texture and temperature. When you chew, aromas rise from the back of the mouth into the nose. That is why food tastes dull when you have a blocked nose. Spicy heat from chili is not a taste at all; it is a pain and heat signal."],["Touch","The skin has different sensors for light touch, pressure, vibration, stretching, heat, cold and pain. Fingertips and lips have the most, so they are the most sensitive. The brain uses touch signals to judge texture, grip objects safely and know where the body is."]],
k:[["Basic tastes","5: sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami"],["Taste buds","Several thousand"],["Smell receptor types","About 400"],["Most touch-sensitive areas","Fingertips and lips"],["Umami named","1908, Kikunae Ikeda"]],
d:[["1824","Louis Braille creates his raised-dot reading system for blind people"],["1901","A misread German study gives rise to the tongue map myth"],["1908","Kikunae Ikeda identifies umami, the fifth basic taste"],["2004","Nobel Prize awarded for discovering how smell receptors work"],["2021","Nobel Prize awarded for discovering receptors for heat and touch"]],
s2:[["A powerful nose","Humans can tell apart a huge number of smells, and our sense of smell is better than once thought. Still, dogs have many more smell receptors, which is why they are used to sniff out drugs, explosives, missing people and even some diseases."],["Why taste matters","Sweet tastes signal energy-rich food, salty tastes signal minerals the body needs, and umami signals protein. Bitter and sour tastes can warn of poisons or spoiled food. Children often dislike bitter vegetables, but tastes can change with age and practice."],["Umami","In 1908 Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda wondered why kombu seaweed broth tasted so good. He found the cause was glutamate and named its taste umami, meaning delicious taste. He went on to develop monosodium glutamate, or MSG, a seasoning now used in cooking around the world."],["Pain","Pain sensors in the skin and organs warn of injury. Pain is unpleasant, but it protects us; people born unable to feel pain often hurt themselves without knowing. The brain can turn pain up or down, which is why distraction and calm can make an injury hurt less."],["Losing a sense","Some people are born without a sense of smell, and others lose it after an illness or injury. During the COVID-19 pandemic, millions of people suddenly lost their sense of smell and taste for weeks or months. Losing smell can make food less enjoyable and makes it harder to notice smoke or gas leaks."],["Reading by touch","Braille is a system of raised dots that blind people read with their fingertips. It was invented in 1824 by Louis Braille, a 15-year-old French student who had lost his sight as a small child. Braille has been adapted for hundreds of languages, including Chinese, Arabic and Hindi, and appears on elevator buttons and medicine boxes."]],
sp:["The chili pepper and the Nobel Prize","Chili peppers feel hot because a chemical in them, capsaicin, switches on the same sensor in our nerves that responds to real heat. Scientist David Julius used capsaicin to find this sensor, and Ardem Patapoutian discovered sensors that respond to pressure. They shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2021."],
g:[["Receptor","A cell or molecule that detects a signal"],["Taste bud","A cluster of cells on the tongue that detects taste"],["Umami","A savory taste, as in broth or cheese"],["Braille","A reading system of raised dots"]],
f:["Every part of the tongue can sense all five basic tastes.","Chili heat is a pain and heat signal, not a taste.","Louis Braille invented Braille at 15."],
fun:"The tongue map showing separate taste zones is a myth."});

ADDART({id:"memory",t:"Memory and Learning",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🧠",w:"Memory",r:["brain","sleep","smelltaste"],
p:[["How we remember","Memory is the brain’s ability to store and recall information and experiences."],["Practice makes permanent","Learning changes the connections between brain cells."],["Sleep helps","During sleep, the brain sorts and strengthens the day’s memories."]],
l:"Memory lets us store what we learn and experience and recall it later. It allows us to recognize faces, speak languages, ride bicycles and remember who we are. Learning physically changes the brain, strengthening connections between nerve cells. Scientists have discovered which brain areas build memories, why we forget and which study methods work best.",
s:[["Kinds of memory","Short-term or working memory holds information for seconds, like a phone number while you dial it. Long-term memory can last a lifetime. It includes memories of events and facts, such as your last birthday or the capital of Japan, and skills, such as swimming or typing, that we do without thinking."],["The hippocampus","Deep inside the brain, a curved structure called the hippocampus is essential for forming new memories of events and facts. Over time, memories are stored more widely across the brain’s outer layer, the cortex. Damage to the hippocampus can stop a person forming new memories."],["Brain cells and learning","The brain has about 86 billion nerve cells, or neurons, linked at junctions called synapses. When we learn, connections that are used together grow stronger, and new ones form. This ability of the brain to change, called neuroplasticity, continues throughout life."],["Forgetting","Most new information fades quickly unless it is reviewed. In 1885 German psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus tested himself on nonsense syllables and drew a forgetting curve, showing that much is forgotten within a day. Each review makes a memory last longer."]],
k:[["Neurons in the brain","About 86 billion"],["Working memory","About 4 to 7 items at once"],["Key brain area","Hippocampus"],["Forgetting curve","Described in 1885"],["Best study tips","Spaced review and self-testing"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","The Greek poet Simonides is said to invent the memory palace method"],["1885","Hermann Ebbinghaus publishes his studies of memory and forgetting"],["1953","Surgery leaves patient H.M. unable to form new memories, teaching scientists about the hippocampus"],["1956","George Miller describes the limits of short-term memory"],["2000","Eric Kandel shares a Nobel Prize for discovering how memories form in nerve cells"]],
s2:[["Patient H.M.","In 1953 a young American man named Henry Molaison had brain surgery to stop severe seizures, and parts of both hippocampi were removed. Afterward he could not form new memories of events, though he could still learn new skills. Studies of H.M. over 55 years showed that different kinds of memory use different brain systems."],["How to study well","Research shows some study habits work much better than others. Spacing out reviews over days beats cramming. Testing yourself, by recalling answers without looking, works better than rereading. Explaining ideas in your own words and mixing different kinds of problems also help."],["Sleep and memory","During deep sleep, the brain replays the day’s experiences and moves memories from the hippocampus into long-term storage. Students who sleep after studying remember more than those who stay up all night. Not getting enough sleep makes it harder to concentrate and learn."],["Memory tricks","Mnemonics help us remember lists. Acronyms, rhymes and songs are mnemonics, as is the sentence My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Noodles for the planets. In the memory palace method, you imagine placing things to remember along a familiar route, like rooms in your house."],["Memory can be wrong","Memories are rebuilt each time we recall them, so they can change or even be invented. Two people who saw the same event may remember it differently, and leading questions can create false memories. This is why investigators must question witnesses carefully."],["Memory and age","Children learn languages and skills remarkably fast. In older age, memory for names and recent events may slow, but knowledge and skills often stay strong. Dementia, such as Alzheimer’s disease, is different from normal aging: it is a disease that steadily damages memory and thinking."]],
sp:["The memory palace","According to legend, the Greek poet Simonides left a banquet hall moments before its roof collapsed. He could identify the victims by remembering where each guest had sat. This inspired the method of loci, or memory palace, in which you link facts to places along a familiar route. Memory champions still use it to memorize thousands of numbers."],
g:[["Hippocampus","A brain area essential for forming new memories"],["Synapse","A junction where two nerve cells pass signals"],["Neuroplasticity","The brain’s ability to change with learning"],["Mnemonic","A trick that helps you remember"]],
f:["The brain has about 86 billion nerve cells.","Testing yourself helps you remember better than rereading.","Sleep helps the brain store new memories."],
fun:"Testing yourself is one of the best ways to remember what you study."});

ADDART({id:"phcinema",t:"Philippine Cinema",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎬",w:"Cinema of the Philippines",r:["cinema","phlit","tv"],
p:[["More than a century of film","Films were first shown in Manila in 1897, and Filipinos began making their own feature films in 1919."],["Golden ages","Philippine cinema flourished in the 1950s and again in the 1970s and 1980s."],["Winning worldwide","Filipino filmmakers and actors have won top prizes at Cannes, Venice and Berlin."]],
l:"Philippine cinema is one of the oldest and liveliest film industries in Asia. Since the first films were shown in Manila in 1897, Filipinos have made thousands of movies, from musicals and action films to comedies and serious dramas. Directors such as Lino Brocka and Lav Diaz have won worldwide acclaim, and going to the movies remains a favorite Filipino pastime.",
s:[["The first films","On 1 January 1897, moving pictures were shown for the first time in Manila, at the Salon de Pertierra on Escolta. Foreign films soon became popular. In 1919 José Nepomuceno made Dalagang Bukid, often called the first Filipino-made feature film, and he became known as the father of Philippine movies."],["The first golden age","In the 1950s, big studios such as LVN Pictures, Sampaguita Pictures and Premiere Productions each made dozens of films a year with their own stars and directors. Films such as Lamberto Avellana’s Anak Dalita won prizes at Asian film festivals. The FAMAS awards began in 1953."],["The second golden age","In the 1970s and early 1980s, directors such as Lino Brocka, Ishmael Bernal, Mike de Leon and Marilou Díaz-Abaya made bold films about poverty, injustice and city life, often under censorship during martial law. Brocka’s Insiang was shown at the Cannes Film Festival in 1978."],["Popular movies","Filipino audiences love romantic comedies, horror, action and family dramas. Holiday releases at the Metro Manila Film Festival, held since 1975, draw huge crowds every December. Love teams, pairs of actors who star together in film after film, are a special feature of Filipino entertainment."]],
k:[["First screening","1 January 1897, Manila"],["First Filipino feature","Dalagang Bukid, 1919"],["Father of Philippine movies","José Nepomuceno"],["Metro Manila Film Festival","Since 1975"],["Golden Lion winner","Lav Diaz, 2016"]],
d:[["1896","The Lumière brothers’ films are shown from Bombay to Buenos Aires"],["1919","Dalagang Bukid, the first Filipino-made feature, is released"],["1978","Lino Brocka’s Insiang is shown at Cannes"],["2009","Brillante Mendoza wins Best Director at Cannes"],["2016","Lav Diaz wins the Golden Lion at Venice; Jaclyn Jose wins Best Actress at Cannes"]],
s2:[["Lino Brocka","Lino Brocka directed more than 60 films, including Maynila sa mga Kuko ng Liwanag (1975), Insiang (1976) and Bayan Ko (1984). His films showed the lives of the urban poor and criticized injustice. He also campaigned for freedom of expression. He was named a National Artist for Film after his death in 1991."],["Independent films","Since the 2000s, digital cameras have made filmmaking cheaper, and independent, or indie, films have flourished. Festivals such as Cinemalaya, begun in 2005, support new directors. Many indie films tell stories from the provinces in regional languages."],["International prizes","Brillante Mendoza won Best Director at Cannes in 2009 for Kinatay. In 2016, Jaclyn Jose won Best Actress at Cannes for Ma’ Rosa, and Lav Diaz won the Golden Lion, the top prize at Venice, for Ang Babaeng Humayo. Lav Diaz is known for very long films, some lasting more than eight hours."],["National Artists","The Order of National Artists honors Filipinos for outstanding work. National Artists for Film include Lamberto Avellana, Gerardo de León, Lino Brocka, Ishmael Bernal, Fernando Poe Jr., Nora Aunor and Marilou Díaz-Abaya. Fernando Poe Jr. was the country’s best-loved action star for decades."],["Stars and songs","Many Filipino film stars are also singers, and movie theme songs often become hits. Stars such as Nora Aunor and Vilma Santos became icons. Today many actors move between films, television dramas and streaming series, and Filipino films are also shown on international streaming platforms."],["Films for history","Some Filipino films retell the nation’s history, such as Heneral Luna (2015), about General Antonio Luna, and its sequel Goyo (2018), about Gregorio del Pilar. Restoration projects, such as the ABS-CBN Film Restoration program, are saving old Filipino films that were fading or decaying."]],
sp:["Manila in the Claws of Light","Lino Brocka’s Maynila sa mga Kuko ng Liwanag (1975) follows Julio, a young man from the province who comes to Manila to find his love and struggles to survive in the city. Based on a novel by Edgardo Reyes, it is often named the greatest Filipino film ever made. It was restored in 2013 and shown again at Cannes."],
g:[["Feature film","A full-length film, usually over an hour long"],["Indie film","A film made outside the big studios"],["Love team","A pair of actors who often star together"],["Censorship","Controlling or banning what may be shown or said"]],
f:["Films were first shown in Manila on 1 January 1897.","Lav Diaz won the Golden Lion at Venice in 2016.","Some of Lav Diaz’s films last more than eight hours."],
fun:"The first films in Manila were shown on 1 January 1897."});

ADDART({id:"tv",t:"Television",c:"Technology",i:"📺",w:"Television",r:["radio","cinema","phcinema"],
p:[["Pictures through the air","Television sends moving pictures and sound to screens in homes."],["A 20th-century invention","The first working television systems were shown in the 1920s."],["From TV sets to streaming","Today people watch on smart TVs, phones and tablets, often over the internet."]],
l:"Television is a system for sending and receiving moving pictures and sound. Developed in the 1920s and 1930s, it spread to most homes in the world in the second half of the 1900s and became the main source of news and entertainment for billions of people. Today television reaches viewers by broadcast, cable, satellite and the internet.",
s:[["How TV works","A television camera turns a scene into an electrical signal by scanning it line by line. The signal is sent by radio waves, cable, satellite or the internet. A television set turns the signal back into a picture made of tiny dots, called pixels, changing many times a second so the picture appears to move."],["Inventors","Many inventors helped create television. In 1926 John Logie Baird of Scotland showed the first working television, which used spinning disks. In 1927 Philo Farnsworth of the United States sent the first fully electronic picture. Electronic television, with no moving parts, soon won out."],["The TV age","Regular TV broadcasts began in Britain in 1936. After World War II, television spread quickly. Color TV began in the United States in 1954. By the 1960s, televisions were in most homes in rich countries, and huge audiences watched events such as the Moon landing in 1969."],["Television spreads","Britain’s BBC began the world’s first regular high-definition television service in 1936. After World War II, television spread quickly: Japan began broadcasting in 1953, India in 1959 and most countries by the 1970s. Today more than 1.7 billion homes have a TV set, and people watch news, sport, dramas and game shows in every language."]],
k:[["First demonstration","1926, John Logie Baird"],["First electronic picture","1927, Philo Farnsworth"],["Longest-running TV show","Meet the Press, United States, since 1947"],["Color TV began","1954, United States"],["Moon landing audience","About 600 million people"]],
d:[["1926","John Logie Baird demonstrates television in London"],["1936","The BBC begins regular television broadcasts"],["1953","The coronation of Queen Elizabeth II is televised"],["1969","About 600 million people watch the Moon landing live"],["2010s","Streaming over the internet becomes a major way to watch TV"]],
s2:[["News on TV","Television changed how people learn about the world. Live pictures of wars, disasters and elections reached homes as they happened. In 1989, viewers around the world watched the Berlin Wall fall, and in Romania’s revolution that year the capture of the national TV station became a turning point."],["Soap operas and telenovelas","Some of television’s most popular programs are long-running dramas. Latin American telenovelas from Mexico, Brazil and Colombia, Turkish dramas, Korean dramas and Filipino teleseryes are exported and dubbed for viewers across Asia, Africa, Europe and the Americas, often for months at a time."],["Variety and game shows","Variety and game shows mix games, music, comedy and dancing, often giving prizes to ordinary people. Sábado Gigante, which ran from 1962 to 2015 in Chile and the United States, was one of the longest-running, and Eat Bulaga in the Philippines has aired since 1979. Such shows are part of daily life in many countries."],["From tubes to flat screens","Early televisions used heavy cathode-ray tubes, glass tubes that fired electrons at a glowing screen. From the 2000s, these were replaced by flat screens using liquid crystals or light-emitting diodes. Picture quality improved from fuzzy black-and-white images to sharp high-definition and 4K."],["Digital television","Old analog TV signals are being replaced by digital broadcasts, which give clearer pictures and fit more channels into the same airwaves. Countries chose different standards: Europe developed DVB, the United States ATSC, Japan ISDB, which was also adopted across much of Latin America, and China DTMB. Set-top boxes let older televisions receive the new signals."],["Streaming","Today millions of people watch shows on demand through internet streaming services and video-sharing sites, on phones as well as TVs. Viewers can watch whenever they like instead of following a fixed schedule. Many TV networks now stream their programs online as well as broadcasting them."]],
sp:["The coronation that sold TV sets","On 2 June 1953, the coronation of Queen Elizabeth II was broadcast live from Westminster Abbey. Hundreds of thousands of British families bought or rented their first television to watch it, and many crowded into neighbors’ homes. About 20 million people in Britain watched, more than listened on the radio, and films of the ceremony were flown across the Atlantic to be shown in North America the same day."],
g:[["Broadcast","To send a program out to many viewers at once"],["Pixel","One tiny dot in a picture on a screen"],["Telenovela","A Latin American television drama that runs for months"],["Streaming","Watching video over the internet as it downloads"]],
f:["About 20 million people in Britain watched the 1953 coronation on television.","John Logie Baird demonstrated television in 1926.","About 600 million people watched the Moon landing live."],
fun:"Philippine television began on 23 October 1953 with station DZAQ-TV Channel 3."});

ADDART({id:"lizard",t:"Lizards",i:"🦎",w:"Komodo dragon",r:["snake","croc","phbio"],cons:["EN","Komodo dragon"],
p:[["The biggest reptile group","Lizards are scaly reptiles, and with snakes they form the largest group of reptiles."],["From tiny to giant","They range from geckos smaller than a coin to the 3-meter Komodo dragon."],["Amazing tricks","Lizards can drop their tails, change color, glide and even run on water."]],
l:"Lizards are scaly, cold-blooded reptiles found on every continent except Antarctica. There are more than 7,000 species, from tiny geckos to the giant Komodo dragon. Lizards have astonishing abilities: geckos climb glass, chameleons change color, flying lizards glide between trees and many species can shed their tails to escape predators.",
s:[["What is a lizard?","Most lizards have four legs, movable eyelids, external ear openings and long tails, which set them apart from snakes. Their skin is covered in dry scales. Like other reptiles, they are cold-blooded, warming themselves in the sun and cooling off in the shade."],["The Komodo dragon","The Komodo dragon of Indonesia is the largest living lizard, reaching about 3 meters long and weighing more than 70 kilograms. It hunts deer, pigs and water buffalo, killing them with sharp teeth and a venomous bite. Only a few thousand remain on a handful of islands."],["Geckos","Geckos are small, often nocturnal lizards. Many have toe pads covered in millions of tiny hairs that let them cling to walls and ceilings, even upside down on glass. The tokay gecko of Asia is named after its loud call."],["Escape artists","Many lizards can drop their tails when grabbed. The wriggling tail distracts the predator while the lizard escapes, and a new, shorter tail slowly grows back. Some lizards rely on camouflage, while others puff up, hiss or squirt blood from their eyes, as horned lizards do."]],
k:[["Species","More than 7,000"],["Largest","Komodo dragon, about 3 m"],["Smallest","Some dwarf geckos and chameleons, under 3 cm"],["Komodo dragons left","A few thousand"],["Fastest","Spiny-tailed iguana, about 35 km/h"]],
d:[["1910","Komodo dragons become known to Western scientists"],["1980","Komodo National Park is created in Indonesia"],["2005","A drug based on Gila monster saliva is approved to treat diabetes"],["2010","A large new fruit-eating monitor lizard, Varanus bitatawa, is described"],["2021","Komodo dragon listed as Endangered by the IUCN"]],
s2:[["Chameleons","Chameleons have eyes that move separately, letting them look in two directions at once, and tongues that shoot out faster than the eye can follow to catch insects. They change color by adjusting tiny crystals in their skin, mostly to signal mood and temperature rather than to hide."],["Flying lizards","Flying lizards of the genus Draco, found in the forests of South and Southeast Asia, have ribs that open like a fan, spreading flaps of skin into wings. They glide from tree to tree, sometimes for more than 8 meters, steering with their tails. Males also flash a colorful throat flap."],["Monitor lizards","Monitors are large, intelligent lizards with long necks and forked tongues. They live in Africa, Asia and Australia, from the Nile monitor to Australia’s perentie. A few, such as Gray’s monitor of the Philippines, eat mostly fruit, and in 2010 scientists described another large fruit-eating monitor, Varanus bitatawa, unknown to science until then."],["Sailfin lizards","Sailfin lizards of Indonesia, New Guinea and the Philippines have a tall crest along the tail and can run across water for short distances, using flattened toes. Basilisks of Central America do the same, earning the name Jesus Christ lizards. Both live near rivers and dive into the water when disturbed."],["Lizards and medicine","The saliva of the Gila monster, a venomous lizard of the North American deserts, contains a substance that helps control blood sugar. Scientists made a copy of it, and in 2005 it was approved as a medicine for type 2 diabetes. Venoms of many animals are studied for new drugs."],["Threats","Lizards face habitat loss, introduced predators such as cats and rats, and capture for the pet trade. Island lizards are especially at risk. Some, such as the Komodo dragon, may also lose habitat to rising seas. Protected areas and laws against illegal trade help save them."]],
sp:["Fruit-eating monitors","Most monitor lizards eat meat, but a few eat mainly fruit. Gray’s monitor, a large, shy lizard of the forests of southern Luzon, feeds mostly on ripe fruit, especially pandan, and spends much of its time high in trees. A close relative, Varanus bitatawa, was described in 2010 and showed that even big animals can go unnoticed by science. Hunting and forest loss threaten both."],
g:[["Cold-blooded","Having a body temperature that depends on the surroundings"],["Autotomy","Dropping a body part, such as a tail, to escape"],["Monitor","A large, long-necked lizard"],["Camouflage","Colors that help an animal blend in"]],
f:["Geckos can cling to glass with millions of tiny toe hairs.","Some lizards can drop their tails and grow new ones.","Flying lizards can glide more than 8 meters between trees."],
fun:"The tokay gecko, or tuko, is named after the sound of its call."});

ADDART({id:"ants",t:"Ants",i:"🐜",w:"Ant",r:["insect","bee","eco","anteaters","plantdefenses"],
p:[["Living in colonies","Ants are social insects that live in colonies, sometimes of millions."],["Teamwork","Workers find food, care for young and defend the nest, all guided by chemical signals."],["Everywhere","There are about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth, around 2.5 million for every person."]],
l:"Ants are small social insects that live in organized colonies ruled by one or more queens. There are more than 14,000 known species, found almost everywhere on land. By working together, ants build nests, farm fungus, herd other insects and defend their homes. They move soil, spread seeds and recycle dead matter, making them some of the most important animals on Earth.",
s:[["The colony","A colony usually has one or more queens that lay eggs, many sterile female workers and, at certain times, winged males and new queens. Workers dig the nest, gather food, feed the young and fight enemies. Some colonies have just a few dozen ants; others have millions."],["Chemical messages","Ants communicate mainly with chemicals called pheromones. A worker that finds food lays a scent trail back to the nest, and others follow it, strengthening the trail. Other pheromones sound alarms, mark nestmates and identify the queen."],["Strength","Ants are very strong for their size. Some can carry objects many times their own weight in their jaws. Working together, groups of ants can move large prey or food crumbs and even build bridges and rafts from their own bodies."],["Life cycle","Ants grow through four stages: egg, larva, pupa and adult. Workers care for the eggs, feed the wormlike larvae and move the young around the nest to keep them at the right temperature and humidity."]],
k:[["Known species","More than 14,000"],["Ants on Earth","About 20 quadrillion"],["Colony size","A few dozen to millions"],["Ancestors","About 140 million years ago"],["Communication","Mainly pheromones"]],
d:[["c. 140 million years ago","The first ants appear"],["1962","E. O. Wilson shows how ants use chemical trails"],["1991","The Ants, by Bert Hölldobler and E. O. Wilson, wins a Pulitzer Prize"],["2002","Scientists report an Argentine ant supercolony stretching 6,000 km across southern Europe"],["2022","A study estimates there are about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth"]],
s2:[["Farmers and herders","Leafcutter ants of the Americas cut leaves, carry them home and use them to grow a fungus garden for food, a farming habit that began millions of years before humans farmed. Some ants protect aphids and scale insects and collect the sweet honeydew they produce, much like herders with cattle."],["Army ants","Army ants have no permanent nest. Huge columns of hundreds of thousands march through the forest, catching insects and other small animals. At night they build a living nest, called a bivouac, from their own linked bodies, with the queen and young inside."],["Weaver ants","Weaver ants build nests by stitching leaves together with silk produced by their larvae. Workers hold larvae in their jaws and use them like glue tubes. Farmers in Asia have used weaver ants for centuries to protect fruit trees, including mango and citrus, from pests."],["Fire ants and stings","Many ants bite or sting to defend their colonies. Fire ants have painful stings and have spread to many countries as invasive pests. The bullet ant of South America is said to have one of the most painful stings of any insect. Most ants, however, are harmless to people."],["Ants and nature","Ants turn over as much soil as earthworms in some places, letting in air and water. They carry seeds into their nests, helping plants spread, and eat many pests. In tropical forests, the total weight of ants can be greater than that of all the mammals and birds."],["Supercolonies","Some ants, such as the Argentine ant, form supercolonies in which millions of nests cooperate instead of fighting. One supercolony stretches about 6,000 kilometers along the coasts of Spain, France and Italy. These invasive ants push out native species."]],
sp:["Weaver ants on the farm","In parts of Asia, farmers place weaver ant nests in mango and citrus trees and link the trees with ropes or bamboo bridges. The ants patrol the branches and eat or chase away pest insects, reducing the need for pesticides. Records of this practice in China go back about 1,700 years, making it one of the oldest known uses of biological pest control."],
g:[["Colony","A group of ants living together"],["Pheromone","A chemical signal used by animals"],["Larva","The wormlike young stage of an insect"],["Supercolony","Many connected nests that cooperate"]],
f:["There are about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth.","Leafcutter ants farm fungus for food.","Weaver ants build nests by stitching leaves with silk."],
fun:"There are about 2.5 million ants for every person on Earth."});

ADDART({id:"tsunami",t:"Tsunamis",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Tsunami",r:["quake","tect","volc"],
p:[["Giant waves","A tsunami is a series of powerful ocean waves caused by a sudden movement of the sea floor."],["Fast as a jet","In the deep ocean, tsunami waves can travel at about 800 kilometers per hour."],["Know the signs","A strong earthquake or the sea suddenly pulling back are warnings to move to high ground."]],
l:"A tsunami is a series of huge ocean waves caused when a large volume of water is suddenly pushed up or down, usually by an undersea earthquake. Tsunamis cross whole oceans in hours and can flood coasts kilometers inland. Coasts around the Pacific Ring of Fire and the Indian Ocean face the greatest risk.",
s:[["What causes tsunamis","Most tsunamis are caused by large earthquakes at subduction zones, where one tectonic plate slides beneath another and the sea floor suddenly lifts or drops. Underwater landslides, volcanic eruptions and, very rarely, meteorite impacts can also cause them. The word tsunami is Japanese for harbor wave."],["How tsunamis travel","In deep water, a tsunami may be less than a meter high and pass under ships unnoticed, but it moves at about 800 km/h, the speed of a jet plane. As it reaches shallow water near the coast, it slows down and piles up, sometimes into walls of water tens of meters high."],["Not like normal waves","Ordinary waves are made by wind and move only the surface. A tsunami moves the whole depth of the ocean, so it carries enormous energy. It often arrives as a fast-rising flood rather than a breaking wave, and the first wave is not always the largest. Waves may keep coming for hours."],["Warning signs","Natural warnings include a strong or long earthquake near the coast, the sea suddenly drawing back to expose the sea floor, and a loud roar from the ocean. If you notice any of these, move immediately to high ground or far inland and stay there until officials say it is safe."]],
k:[["Speed in deep ocean","About 800 km/h"],["Main cause","Undersea earthquakes"],["Deadliest","2004 Indian Ocean, about 230,000 deaths"],["Deadliest recorded","2004 Indian Ocean, about 230,000 deaths"],["Warning centers","Pacific and Indian Ocean warning systems"]],
d:[["1960","The largest earthquake ever recorded, in Chile, sends a tsunami across the Pacific"],["1755","An earthquake and tsunami devastate Lisbon, Portugal"],["2004","The Indian Ocean tsunami kills about 230,000 people in 14 countries"],["2011","The Tōhoku tsunami strikes Japan and damages the Fukushima nuclear plant"],["2022","The Hunga Tonga eruption sends tsunami waves around the Pacific"]],
s2:[["The Indian Ocean tsunami","On 26 December 2004, a magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra, Indonesia, set off a tsunami that struck coasts around the Indian Ocean. About 230,000 people died in 14 countries, including Indonesia, Sri Lanka, India and Thailand. There was no warning system in the Indian Ocean then; one was set up afterward."],["The Moro Gulf tsunami","On the morning of 1 November 1755, a great earthquake off Portugal shook Lisbon, and about 40 minutes later tsunami waves swept into the city, while fires burned for days. Waves also struck Spain and Morocco. Tens of thousands of people died. The disaster shocked Europe, led to some of the first earthquake-resistant buildings, and is seen as the start of modern seismology."],["Japan, 2011","On 11 March 2011, a magnitude 9.0 to 9.1 earthquake off northeastern Japan sent waves up to about 40 meters high onto the coast. Nearly 20,000 people died, and the tsunami flooded the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, causing a serious nuclear accident."],["Warning systems","Networks of seismometers and ocean buoys detect earthquakes and changes in sea level, and warning centers such as the Pacific Tsunami Warning Center can alert coasts within minutes. National agencies tell coastal communities when to evacuate. Sirens, text alerts and marked evacuation routes help people move to safety."],["Megatsunamis","Rare landslides into narrow bays can cause enormous local waves called megatsunamis. In 1958 an earthquake in Lituya Bay, Alaska, dropped rock into the water and sent a wave surging 524 meters up the opposite slope, stripping away the forest. Far from the landslide, the wave was much smaller."],["Being prepared","People in coastal areas should know their evacuation route and nearby high ground, prepare an emergency bag and practice drills. Planting mangroves and keeping coral reefs healthy can help reduce wave damage. Most importantly, after a strong coastal earthquake, do not wait for an official warning; move to high ground at once."]],
sp:["Saved by a geography lesson","In 2004, ten-year-old Tilly Smith was on holiday on a beach in Thailand when she saw the sea bubbling and pulling back. She remembered a geography lesson about tsunamis and warned her parents, who alerted others. The beach was cleared before the waves hit, and about 100 people were saved."],
g:[["Tsunami","A series of huge waves caused by a sudden movement of water"],["Subduction zone","Where one tectonic plate slides under another"],["Evacuate","To leave a dangerous place for a safe one"],["Seismometer","An instrument that measures earthquakes"]],
f:["The word tsunami means harbor wave in Japanese.","Tsunamis can cross oceans at about 800 km/h.","The first tsunami wave is not always the largest."],
fun:"A tsunami in the deep ocean can travel as fast as a jet plane."});

;
/* data/gaps1.js */
// Gap articles, part 1: Space (the Sun, Mercury, Venus, Uranus and Neptune, Pluto, the Apollo Moon landings).
ADDART({id:"sun",t:"The Sun",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"☀️",w:"Sun",r:["solar","stars","eclipse","hrdiagram"],
p:[["Our star","The Sun is a star, a huge ball of hot, glowing gas at the center of the Solar System."],["Source of life","Its light and heat power the weather, plants and almost all life on Earth."],["Big and far","The Sun is about 109 times wider than Earth and about 150 million kilometers away."]],
l:"The Sun is the star at the heart of our Solar System. A gigantic ball of hydrogen and helium, it holds more than 99.8 percent of the Solar System’s mass and shines by nuclear fusion deep in its core. Its light, which takes about eight minutes to reach us, warms our planet, drives its weather and makes life on Earth possible.",
s:[["A middle-aged star","The Sun formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust. Astronomers call it a yellow dwarf, an ordinary, medium-sized star. It is about halfway through its life and will keep shining steadily for about another 5 billion years."],["Nuclear fusion","In the Sun’s core, where the temperature is about 15 million °C, hydrogen nuclei are squeezed together to form helium. This fusion turns about 4 million metric tons of matter into energy every second. The energy slowly works its way out and leaves the surface as light and heat."],["Layers","The Sun has no solid surface. Its visible surface, the photosphere, is about 5,500 °C. Above it lie the chromosphere and the corona, the Sun’s outer atmosphere, which is strangely hotter, more than a million degrees. The corona can be seen as a pale glow during a total solar eclipse."],["Sunspots and storms","Sunspots are cooler, darker patches caused by tangled magnetic fields. Their number rises and falls in a cycle of about 11 years. Near sunspots, huge explosions called solar flares and clouds of gas called coronal mass ejections can hurl particles toward Earth."]],
k:[["Age","About 4.6 billion years"],["Diameter","About 1.39 million km, 109 × Earth"],["Distance from Earth","About 150 million km"],["Core temperature","About 15 million °C"],["Light travel time","About 8 minutes 20 seconds"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo and others observe sunspots through telescopes"],["1859","The Carrington Event, a giant solar storm, disrupts telegraphs"],["1868","Helium is discovered in the Sun’s light before it is found on Earth"],["2018","NASA launches the Parker Solar Probe"],["2024","Parker Solar Probe passes about 6 million km from the Sun’s surface"]],
s2:[["The solar wind","The Sun constantly blows out a stream of charged particles called the solar wind, which flows past all the planets. Earth’s magnetic field shields us from most of it. Where the particles reach the upper atmosphere near the poles, they make the glowing curtains of the auroras."],["Space weather","Big solar storms can damage satellites, disrupt radio and GPS signals and overload power grids. In 1859 the Carrington Event made telegraph wires spark and produced auroras seen near the equator. Scientists now watch the Sun constantly to give warnings of space weather."],["Never look at the Sun","Looking directly at the Sun, even briefly, can permanently damage your eyes, and looking through binoculars or a telescope without a proper solar filter can blind you instantly. Safe ways to view the Sun include certified eclipse glasses and making a pinhole projector."],["Touching the Sun","NASA’s Parker Solar Probe, launched in 2018, flies through the Sun’s corona protected by a carbon heat shield. On 24 December 2024 it passed about 6 million kilometers from the Sun’s surface, traveling at about 690,000 kilometers per hour, the fastest any human-made object has ever gone."],["The Sun’s future","In about 5 billion years, the Sun will run low on hydrogen in its core. It will swell into a red giant, perhaps large enough to swallow Mercury and Venus and scorch Earth. Later it will shed its outer layers and shrink into a small, dim white dwarf."],["The Sun in culture","Many cultures have worshipped the Sun, from Ra in ancient Egypt to Inti of the Inca and Amaterasu in Japan. The Sun appears on the flags of many countries, from Japan and Argentina to Kazakhstan and Malawi. Solar power now turns sunlight directly into electricity in homes around the world."]],
sp:["The Sun on flags","The Sun is one of the most popular symbols on national flags. Japan’s flag is a red sun on a white field, and the country is called the land of the rising sun. The Sun of May shines on the flags of Argentina and Uruguay, a golden sun with an eagle flies on Kazakhstan’s flag, and suns also appear on the flags of Kyrgyzstan, Malawi, Namibia, Nepal, the Philippines, Rwanda and Taiwan."],
g:[["Nuclear fusion","Joining atomic nuclei together, releasing energy"],["Corona","The Sun’s hot outer atmosphere"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles flowing from the Sun"],["Sunspot","A cooler, darker patch on the Sun"]],
f:["The Sun holds more than 99.8 percent of the Solar System’s mass.","Sunlight takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth.","Helium was found in the Sun before it was found on Earth."],
fun:"Helium was discovered in sunlight in 1868, decades before it was found on Earth."});

ADDART({id:"mercury",t:"Mercury",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"☿️",w:"Mercury (planet)",r:["solar","venus","sun"],
p:[["Closest to the Sun","Mercury is the planet closest to the Sun and the smallest planet in the Solar System."],["The fastest planet","It races around the Sun in just 88 Earth days."],["Fire and ice","Its surface swings between scorching heat and bitter cold."]],
l:"Mercury is the smallest planet and the one closest to the Sun. A rocky world covered in craters, it looks much like our Moon. With almost no atmosphere to hold in heat, it bakes at more than 400 °C by day and freezes far below zero at night. Only two spacecraft have studied it closely, and a third is on its way.",
s:[["Size and orbit","Mercury is about 4,880 kilometers wide, only a little bigger than our Moon. It orbits the Sun at an average distance of about 58 million kilometers, completing a year in just 88 Earth days. It was named after the swift messenger of the Roman gods."],["Long days","Mercury spins slowly. From one sunrise to the next takes about 176 Earth days, so a single day on Mercury lasts two of its years. Someone standing in certain places could watch the Sun rise, stop, go backward briefly and then continue across the sky."],["Extreme temperatures","Without a thick atmosphere, Mercury cannot keep its heat. Daytime temperatures reach about 430 °C, hot enough to melt lead, while nighttime temperatures drop to about −180 °C. It is not the hottest planet, however; Venus is hotter."],["A big iron heart","Mercury has a huge iron core that takes up most of its inside, much larger in proportion than Earth’s. As the core slowly cooled, the planet shrank, wrinkling the surface into long cliffs hundreds of kilometers long."]],
k:[["Diameter","About 4,880 km"],["Distance from Sun","About 58 million km"],["Year","88 Earth days"],["Day (sunrise to sunrise)","About 176 Earth days"],["Moons","None"]],
d:[["1631","Pierre Gassendi observes a transit of Mercury across the Sun"],["1974","Mariner 10 makes the first flyby of Mercury"],["2011","MESSENGER becomes the first spacecraft to orbit Mercury"],["2012","MESSENGER confirms water ice in shadowed polar craters"],["2018","The European–Japanese BepiColombo mission is launched to Mercury"]],
s2:[["Craters","Mercury’s surface is covered in craters from billions of years of impacts. The largest, the Caloris Basin, is about 1,500 kilometers wide. The impact that formed it was so powerful that it jumbled the ground on the opposite side of the planet. Many craters are named after artists, writers and composers."],["Ice on a hot planet","Surprisingly, Mercury has ice. At its poles, the floors of some deep craters never see sunlight and stay colder than −170 °C. In 2012 the MESSENGER spacecraft confirmed that these craters hold water ice, probably delivered by comets and asteroids."],["Visiting Mercury","Mercury is hard to reach because a spacecraft falling toward the Sun speeds up and must brake hard to stay near the planet. Mariner 10 flew past three times in 1974 and 1975. MESSENGER orbited from 2011 to 2015, and BepiColombo, launched in 2018, entered orbit around Mercury on 21 November 2026."],["Seeing Mercury","Because it stays close to the Sun in the sky, Mercury can only be seen low near the horizon shortly after sunset or before sunrise. Many people have never noticed it. Every few years it passes directly across the face of the Sun, an event called a transit."],["A thin exosphere","Mercury has only a very thin layer of atoms around it, called an exosphere, made of particles knocked off the surface by sunlight and the solar wind. It also has a weak magnetic field, which surprised scientists because the planet is so small."],["Could people live there?","Mercury’s extreme heat, cold, radiation and lack of air make it one of the worst places for people in the Solar System. However, some scientists have suggested that the edges of polar craters, with ice nearby and constant sunlight on the rims, might one day host robotic stations."]],
sp:["A day longer than a year","On Mercury, one full day from sunrise to sunrise lasts about 176 Earth days, while one year lasts only 88. So a Mercury day is twice as long as its year. Venus is even stranger: it spins backward so slowly that its day is longer than its year too."],
g:[["Orbit","The path of a planet around the Sun"],["Transit","When a planet passes in front of the Sun as seen from Earth"],["Exosphere","A very thin outer layer of atoms around a planet"],["Crater","A bowl-shaped hole made by an impact"]],
f:["Mercury has no moons.","A day on Mercury lasts two of its years.","There is water ice in Mercury’s polar craters."],
fun:"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, yet it has ice in its polar craters."});

ADDART({id:"venus",t:"Venus",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌕",w:"Venus",r:["solar","mercury","climate"],
p:[["Earth’s twin?","Venus is almost the same size as Earth, but it is a very different world."],["The hottest planet","Its thick atmosphere traps heat, making it hotter than Mercury."],["Morning and evening star","Venus is the brightest planet in our sky."]],
l:"Venus is the second planet from the Sun and Earth’s nearest planetary neighbor. It is nearly the same size as Earth, but beneath its thick, cloudy atmosphere lies a scorching, crushing world with surface temperatures of about 465 °C. Venus shines brilliantly in the morning and evening sky and has fascinated people since ancient times.",
s:[["A runaway greenhouse","Venus’s atmosphere is about 96 percent carbon dioxide and is so thick that the pressure at the surface is about 92 times that on Earth, like being 900 meters deep in the ocean. The carbon dioxide traps heat in a runaway greenhouse effect, making Venus the hottest planet, hot enough to melt lead."],["Clouds of acid","Venus is wrapped in thick clouds of sulfuric acid droplets that hide its surface from view. The clouds reflect most sunlight, which is why Venus looks so bright. High winds blow the clouds around the whole planet in about four Earth days."],["Spinning backward","Venus rotates very slowly and in the opposite direction to most planets, so on Venus the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east. One rotation takes about 243 Earth days, longer than its year of about 225 days."],["The surface","Radar can see through the clouds. It shows a surface of volcanic plains, thousands of volcanoes, mountains and long lava channels. Scientists think Venus may still have active volcanoes. There is no liquid water, and the ground glows dull red in the heat."]],
k:[["Diameter","About 12,100 km"],["Surface temperature","About 465 °C"],["Surface pressure","About 92 × Earth’s"],["Year","About 225 Earth days"],["Rotation","About 243 Earth days, backward"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo sees the phases of Venus, supporting a Sun-centered Solar System"],["1962","Mariner 2 makes the first successful flyby of another planet, Venus"],["1970","Venera 7 makes the first soft landing on another planet and sends data from Venus’s surface"],["1982","Venera 13 sends back the first color photographs from Venus’s surface"],["1990","Magellan begins mapping Venus by radar"]],
s2:[["Galileo’s clue","In 1610 Galileo saw through his telescope that Venus shows phases like the Moon, from thin crescent to nearly full. This could only happen if Venus orbited the Sun, strong evidence for Copernicus’s idea that the planets circle the Sun rather than Earth."],["Landing on Venus","The Soviet Union’s Venera probes were the first to land on another planet. Venera 7 reached the surface in 1970 and sent signals for 23 minutes. Venera 13 survived for about two hours in 1982, taking color pictures of a rocky, orange-lit landscape before the heat and pressure destroyed it."],["What went wrong?","Venus and Earth formed at about the same time and may once have been similar. Some scientists think Venus once had shallow oceans. As the young Sun grew brighter, Venus’s water may have evaporated, letting carbon dioxide build up and heat run away. Studying Venus helps scientists understand climate change on Earth."],["The morning star","Venus is the brightest object in the night sky after the Moon. Because it is closer to the Sun than Earth, it is seen only in the east before sunrise or in the west after sunset. Many cultures thought it was two different stars. The Maya tracked Venus so carefully that their calendars predicted its appearances for centuries."],["Transits of Venus","Rarely, Venus passes directly across the face of the Sun. These transits come in pairs eight years apart, separated by more than a century. In the 1700s, scientists traveled the world to time transits and work out the distance to the Sun. The last were in 2004 and 2012; the next will be in 2117."],["New missions","After decades with few visits, several new missions to Venus are planned for the 2030s, including NASA’s VERITAS and DAVINCI and Europe’s EnVision. They aim to map the surface in detail, study the atmosphere and find out whether Venus’s volcanoes are still active."]],
sp:["A day on Venus","Venus turns so slowly, and in the opposite direction from Earth, that its day is longer than its year. If you could stand on its surface, the Sun would rise in the west and set in the east, but you would never see it through the thick clouds. Combined with the planet’s orbit, one sunrise to the next takes about 117 Earth days."],
g:[["Greenhouse effect","The trapping of heat by gases in an atmosphere"],["Phases","The changing shapes of a lit world as seen from Earth"],["Radar","Using radio waves to detect and map objects"],["Atmospheric pressure","The weight of air pressing down on a surface"]],
f:["Venus is hotter than Mercury.","On Venus the Sun rises in the west.","A day on Venus is longer than its year."],
fun:"Venus is the only planet where the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east."});

ADDART({id:"uranep",t:"Uranus and Neptune",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔵",w:"Neptune",r:["solar","saturn","jupiter"],
p:[["The ice giants","Uranus and Neptune are the two outermost planets, called ice giants."],["Found with telescopes","They were the first planets discovered with telescopes, in 1781 and 1846."],["One visitor","Only one spacecraft, Voyager 2, has ever flown past them."]],
l:"Uranus and Neptune are the seventh and eighth planets from the Sun, cold blue-green worlds at the edge of the planetary system. Called ice giants, they are smaller than Jupiter and Saturn but still about four times wider than Earth. Uranus spins on its side, and Neptune has the fastest winds in the Solar System. Both remain among the least explored planets.",
s:[["Ice giants","Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants made mostly of hydrogen and helium. Uranus and Neptune have thick layers of water, ammonia and methane ices around rocky cores, beneath atmospheres of hydrogen, helium and methane. Methane absorbs red light, giving both planets their blue colors."],["Uranus on its side","Uranus is tilted about 98 degrees, so it rolls around the Sun on its side. Each pole gets about 42 years of continuous sunlight followed by 42 years of darkness during its 84-year orbit. Scientists think a giant collision long ago may have knocked it over."],["Neptune’s winds","Neptune is the windiest planet, with winds up to about 2,000 kilometers per hour, faster than the speed of sound on Earth. In 1989 Voyager 2 photographed a huge storm called the Great Dark Spot, which had vanished when the Hubble Space Telescope looked a few years later."],["Rings and moons","Both planets have thin, dark rings and many moons. Uranus’s moons are named after characters from Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, such as Miranda, Titania and Oberon. Neptune’s largest moon, Triton, orbits backward and has geysers of nitrogen, suggesting it was captured from the Kuiper Belt."]],
k:[["Distance from Sun","Uranus about 2.9 billion km; Neptune about 4.5 billion km"],["Year","Uranus 84 Earth years; Neptune 165 Earth years"],["Diameter","About 51,000 km and 49,500 km"],["Tilt of Uranus","About 98 degrees"],["Fastest winds","Neptune, about 2,000 km/h"]],
d:[["1781","William Herschel discovers Uranus"],["1846","Neptune is found where mathematics predicted it"],["1977","Rings around Uranus are discovered"],["1986","Voyager 2 flies past Uranus"],["1989","Voyager 2 flies past Neptune"]],
s2:[["Discovering Uranus","On 13 March 1781, the astronomer William Herschel, observing from his garden in Bath, England, spotted a fuzzy object that moved against the stars. It was the first planet discovered with a telescope, doubling the known size of the Solar System. Herschel wanted to name it after King George III."],["Finding Neptune by math","Astronomers noticed that Uranus was being pulled slightly off course. Urbain Le Verrier in France calculated where an unseen planet must be. On 23 September 1846, Johann Galle in Berlin pointed a telescope there and found Neptune within one degree of the prediction, a triumph of science."],["Voyager 2","Voyager 2, launched in 1977, is the only spacecraft to have visited the ice giants. It flew past Uranus in 1986, finding new moons and rings, and past Neptune in 1989, revealing Triton’s geysers. It is now in interstellar space, more than 20 billion kilometers from Earth."],["Diamond rain","Deep inside Uranus and Neptune, the pressure is so great that carbon from methane may be squeezed into diamonds that sink toward the core like rain. Laboratory experiments that recreate these conditions with powerful lasers have produced tiny diamonds, supporting the idea."],["A long year","Neptune takes 165 Earth years to orbit the Sun. In 2011 it completed its first full orbit since its discovery in 1846. Uranus, at 84 years, has a year about as long as a human life."],["Future missions","Because so little is known about the ice giants, scientists in the United States have named a mission to orbit Uranus and drop a probe into its atmosphere as a top priority. Such a mission could launch in the 2030s and take more than a decade to arrive."]],
sp:["The planet found on paper","Neptune is the only planet found by mathematics before it was seen. Le Verrier sent his prediction to Johann Galle at the Berlin Observatory, who found Neptune on the very night the letter arrived, 23 September 1846. In fact, Galileo had seen Neptune in 1612 and 1613 but thought it was a star."],
g:[["Ice giant","A giant planet made largely of water, ammonia and methane ices"],["Axial tilt","How far a planet is tipped over"],["Geyser","A fountain of liquid or gas bursting from the ground"],["Interstellar space","The space between the stars, beyond the Sun’s influence"]],
f:["Uranus spins on its side.","Neptune was found by mathematics before it was seen.","Neptune’s winds reach about 2,000 km/h."],
fun:"Galileo saw Neptune in 1612 but thought it was a star."});

ADDART({id:"pluto",t:"Pluto and the Dwarf Planets",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Pluto",r:["solar","comet","uranep","dwarfplanets"],
p:[["From planet to dwarf planet","Pluto was called the ninth planet from 1930 until 2006, when it was reclassified as a dwarf planet."],["A heart of ice","In 2015 the New Horizons spacecraft revealed a huge heart-shaped plain on Pluto."],["Many small worlds","Ceres, Eris, Haumea and Makemake are also dwarf planets."]],
l:"Pluto is a small, icy world in the Kuiper Belt beyond Neptune. Discovered in 1930 and long counted as the ninth planet, it was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006. In 2015 a spacecraft finally revealed Pluto’s surface: mountains of ice, glaciers of frozen nitrogen and a giant heart-shaped plain. It is one of several dwarf planets in the Solar System.",
s:[["Discovery","Pluto was discovered on 18 February 1930 by Clyde Tombaugh, a young astronomer at Lowell Observatory in Arizona, who compared photographs of the sky taken days apart to find a dot that moved. The name Pluto, the Roman god of the underworld, was suggested by Venetia Burney, an 11-year-old girl in England."],["Why Pluto is a dwarf planet","In 2005 astronomers found Eris, a distant world about as big as Pluto. Rather than add more and more planets, the International Astronomical Union decided in 2006 that a planet must clear its orbit of other objects. Pluto shares its zone with many Kuiper Belt objects, so it became a dwarf planet."],["Size","Pluto is about 2,377 kilometers wide, smaller than Earth’s Moon. It takes 248 Earth years to orbit the Sun on a stretched, tilted path that sometimes brings it closer than Neptune. Its surface temperature is about −230 °C."],["Pluto’s moons","Pluto has five known moons. The largest, Charon, is about half Pluto’s width, so big that the two orbit a point in space between them, like a double world. The four small moons are Nix, Hydra, Kerberos and Styx."]],
k:[["Discovered","18 February 1930, by Clyde Tombaugh"],["Diameter","About 2,377 km"],["Year","248 Earth years"],["Moons","5"],["Reclassified","24 August 2006"]],
d:[["1930","Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto"],["1978","Pluto’s largest moon, Charon, is discovered"],["2005","Eris, a dwarf planet as large as Pluto, is discovered"],["2006","Pluto is reclassified as a dwarf planet"],["2015","New Horizons flies past Pluto on 14 July"]],
s2:[["New Horizons","NASA’s New Horizons spacecraft launched in 2006 and flew past Pluto on 14 July 2015, after a journey of nine and a half years. Its pictures showed a surprisingly active world, with mountains of water ice as high as the Rockies, nitrogen glaciers and a thin, hazy blue atmosphere."],["The heart","Pluto’s most famous feature is a bright, heart-shaped region named Tombaugh Regio. Its western half, Sputnik Planitia, is a vast plain of frozen nitrogen, carbon monoxide and methane, slowly churning like a lava lamp. It has no craters, showing that its surface is young."],["The Kuiper Belt","Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, a wide ring of icy bodies left over from the formation of the Solar System. Pluto is one of its largest members. On 1 January 2019, New Horizons flew past a small Kuiper Belt object called Arrokoth, which looks like a flattened snowman."],["Other dwarf planets","Ceres is the largest object in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter and was visited by NASA’s Dawn spacecraft from 2015. Eris, Haumea and Makemake orbit beyond Neptune. Haumea spins so fast that it is stretched into an egg shape and has its own ring."],["The planet debate","Many people were upset when Pluto lost its planet status, and some scientists still argue that the definition is too strict. Supporters of the decision say it reflects how many Pluto-like worlds exist. Either way, Pluto remains one of the most interesting places in the Solar System."],["Tombaugh’s journey","Clyde Tombaugh died in 1997. Some of his ashes were placed on the New Horizons spacecraft, which carried them past the world he discovered. The spacecraft is now heading out of the Solar System, so part of Tombaugh is traveling toward the stars."]],
sp:["Named by a schoolgirl","In March 1930, 11-year-old Venetia Burney of Oxford, England, heard about the new planet at breakfast and suggested the name Pluto, after the god of the underworld, because it was so dark and cold. Her grandfather passed the idea to an astronomer, and Lowell Observatory chose it. A dust counter on New Horizons is named after her."],
g:[["Dwarf planet","A round world orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit"],["Kuiper Belt","A ring of icy bodies beyond Neptune"],["Glacier","A slowly flowing mass of ice"],["Reclassify","To place in a different group"]],
f:["Pluto is smaller than Earth’s Moon.","Pluto was named by an 11-year-old girl.","Some of Pluto’s discoverer’s ashes flew past it on New Horizons."],
fun:"Pluto was named by Venetia Burney, an 11-year-old girl in England."});

ADDART({id:"apollo",t:"The Apollo Moon Landings",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👨‍🚀",w:"Apollo program",r:["moon","space","armstrong"],
p:[["To the Moon","NASA’s Apollo program landed 12 astronauts on the Moon between 1969 and 1972."],["One giant leap","On 20 July 1969, Neil Armstrong became the first person to walk on the Moon."],["Rocks and knowledge","Astronauts brought back 382 kilograms of Moon rock, transforming our understanding of the Moon."]],
l:"The Apollo program was the United States’ effort to land people on the Moon and bring them safely home. Between 1969 and 1972, six Apollo missions landed on the Moon, and 12 astronauts walked on its surface. Born of the Cold War space race, Apollo became one of the greatest feats of science and engineering in history, watched by hundreds of millions of people around the world.",
s:[["A challenge","On 25 May 1961, US President John F. Kennedy challenged the nation to land a man on the Moon and return him safely to Earth before the end of the decade. The Soviet Union had just launched the first person into space, and the United States wanted to show its strength."],["The machines","Apollo used the giant Saturn V rocket, a command module in which three astronauts traveled, a service module with fuel and power, and a lunar module that carried two astronauts down to the surface and back up. About 400,000 people worked on the program."],["Apollo 11","On 20 July 1969, the lunar module Eagle landed on the Sea of Tranquility with Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin, while Michael Collins orbited above. Armstrong stepped onto the surface saying, That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind. About 600 million people watched on television."],["Later missions","Apollo 12, 14, 15, 16 and 17 also landed. The last three carried an electric Lunar Roving Vehicle, letting astronauts drive many kilometers. Apollo 17’s Eugene Cernan, in December 1972, was the last person to walk on the Moon. No one has returned since."]],
k:[["Years","1961–1972"],["Moon landings","6"],["Moonwalkers","12"],["Moon rock returned","382 kg"],["First landing","20 July 1969, Apollo 11"]],
d:[["1961","President Kennedy sets the goal of landing on the Moon"],["1967","A fire during a test kills the three Apollo 1 astronauts"],["1968","Apollo 8 becomes the first crewed spacecraft to orbit the Moon"],["1969","Apollo 11 lands on the Moon on 20 July"],["1972","Apollo 17, the last Moon landing, returns to Earth"]],
s2:[["Apollo 1","On 27 January 1967, during a launch rehearsal, a fire broke out inside the Apollo 1 capsule, killing astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White and Roger Chaffee. The tragedy led to major safety redesigns of the spacecraft, which made the later missions possible."],["Earthrise","On 24 December 1968, Apollo 8’s crew became the first people to orbit the Moon and see Earth rise over its horizon. Bill Anders’s photograph Earthrise, showing our blue planet floating in black space, became one of the most famous pictures ever taken and inspired the environmental movement."],["Apollo 13","In April 1970, an oxygen tank exploded on Apollo 13 on the way to the Moon. Astronaut Jack Swigert reported, Houston, we’ve had a problem. The crew used their lunar module as a lifeboat, swung around the Moon and returned safely to Earth, thanks to brilliant work by the crew and mission control."],["What we learned","Apollo’s Moon rocks showed that the Moon is about 4.5 billion years old and probably formed from debris after a Mars-sized body crashed into the young Earth. Astronauts also left experiments, including mirrors that scientists still bounce lasers off to measure the Moon’s distance to within millimeters."],["Moon rocks around the world","After Apollo 11 and Apollo 17, the United States gave small pieces of Moon rock to more than 130 countries and to every US state as goodwill gifts. Many are displayed in museums, but some of these gifts have since been lost or stolen."],["Back to the Moon","No one has walked on the Moon since 1972. NASA’s Artemis program aims to return astronauts to the Moon, including the first woman, and build a long-term presence there; its first uncrewed test flight went around the Moon in 2022, and in April 2026 the crewed Artemis II mission flew four astronauts around the Moon and back. China, India and other countries have also landed robotic spacecraft on the Moon."]],
sp:["Did they really go?","Some people claim the Moon landings were faked. The evidence that they happened is overwhelming: hundreds of kilograms of Moon rock studied by scientists worldwide, mirrors on the Moon still used today, independent tracking of the spacecraft by other countries, including the Soviet Union, and photographs of the landing sites taken by later orbiting spacecraft."],
g:[["Lunar module","The spacecraft that landed astronauts on the Moon"],["Command module","The capsule in which Apollo astronauts traveled and returned to Earth"],["Mission control","The team on the ground that guides a space mission"],["Space race","The Cold War rivalry between the US and the Soviet Union in space"]],
f:["Twelve people have walked on the Moon.","Apollo astronauts brought back 382 kg of Moon rock.","Mirrors left by Apollo astronauts are still used to measure the Moon’s distance."],
fun:"Only 12 people have ever walked on the Moon, all between 1969 and 1972."});

