/* data/gaps2.js */
// Gap articles, part 2: The Philippines (pre-colonial Philippines, baybayin, Indigenous peoples, Martial Law,
// Filipinos overseas, Tubbataha Reefs, Mount Apo, government and Constitution).
ADDART({id:"precol",t:"Pre-colonial Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"⛵",w:"History of the Philippines (900–1565)",r:["baybayin","lapulapu","sulu"],
p:[["Before Spain","For tens of thousands of years before the Spanish arrived in 1521, people lived, farmed, traded and built communities across the islands."],["Barangays and kingdoms","Most people lived in villages called barangays led by datus, while larger trading states grew in places such as Tondo, Cebu, Butuan and Sulu."],["Connected to Asia","Islanders traded gold, pearls and forest goods with China, Southeast Asia and beyond."]],
l:"Long before the Spanish arrived, the Philippine islands were home to thriving communities. Early humans lived on Luzon at least 50,000 years ago, and later Austronesian seafarers brought rice farming, boats and languages. By the year 1000, chiefdoms and trading states were exchanging goods with China and the rest of Southeast Asia, writing in their own scripts and building a rich culture that still shapes Filipino life.",
s:[["The first people","The oldest human remains found in the Philippines come from Callao Cave in Cagayan, where scientists discovered bones of a small human species, Homo luzonensis, at least 50,000 years old. Remains from the Tabon Caves in Palawan show that modern humans lived there tens of thousands of years ago."],["Austronesian seafarers","Around 4,000 to 5,000 years ago, Austronesian-speaking peoples spread through the islands, bringing pottery, farming, domesticated animals and skilled boat building. Their languages are the ancestors of Tagalog, Cebuano, Ilocano and the other languages of the Philippines."],["The barangay","Most early Filipinos lived in barangays, communities of a few dozen to a few hundred families. The word comes from balangay, the plank boats in which early settlers traveled. Each barangay was led by a datu. Society had nobles, freemen and dependents who owed labor or service."],["Trading states","Larger communities grew into trading centers. Chinese records mention traders from Ma-i in 971 and envoys from Butuan in 1001. Tondo and Maynila on Manila Bay, the Rajahnate of Cebu and the Sultanate of Sulu traded with China, Brunei, Java and Malacca."]],
k:[["Earliest humans","At least 50,000 years ago, Callao Cave"],["Austronesian arrival","About 4,000–5,000 years ago"],["Oldest written document","Laguna Copperplate Inscription, 900 CE"],["Oldest boats found","Butuan balangays, from about 320 CE"],["Basic community","The barangay, led by a datu"]],
d:[["c. 50,000 years ago","Homo luzonensis lives in Callao Cave, Cagayan"],["c. 800 BCE","The Manunggul Jar is made in Palawan"],["c. 2500 BCE","The Khufu ship is built in Egypt"],["900 CE","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is written"],["c. 1380","Islam reaches Sulu; a mosque is built on Simunul Island"]],
s2:[["The Laguna Copperplate","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription, found in 1989 near Lumban, Laguna, is the oldest known written document from the Philippines. Dated to 900 CE, it is written in Kawi script and records that a man named Namwaran and his family were freed from a debt. It mentions places such as Tondo and shows links with Java."],["The Manunggul Jar","The Manunggul Jar, a burial jar from a cave in Palawan made around 800 BCE, has a lid showing two figures rowing a boat, believed to carry a soul to the afterlife. It is a National Cultural Treasure and appears on the old 1,000-peso banknote. It shows early Filipinos’ beliefs and artistry."],["Gold","Early Filipinos were master goldsmiths. Hoards found in Surigao and Butuan include belts, chains, sashes and jewelry of remarkable skill made between the 900s and 1200s. The Golden Tara, a gold statue of a Hindu-Buddhist goddess found in Agusan in 1917, shows influences from India and Java."],["Beliefs","Most early Filipinos honored spirits called anito and diwata and their ancestors. Spiritual leaders called babaylan, often women, led rituals, healed the sick and kept community traditions. Islam spread to Sulu and Mindanao from the 1300s, and the Sultanates of Sulu and Maguindanao became powerful states."],["Life and culture","Early Filipinos grew rice and root crops, fished, hunted and wove cloth. Many Visayans covered their bodies with tattoos, which the Spanish called pintados, the painted ones. Epics, songs, riddles and proverbs were passed down orally, and many people could read and write in baybayin."],["Arrival of Europe","In 1521 Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition reached the islands, and Lapulapu of Mactan defeated him in battle. In 1565 Miguel López de Legazpi began the Spanish colonization of the islands, and in 1571 he took Maynila from Rajah Sulayman. The Spanish period that followed lasted more than 300 years."]],
sp:["The balangay boats","In 1976 archaeologists in Butuan, Agusan del Norte, began uncovering the remains of large wooden plank boats called balangay, the oldest dated to about 320 CE. Built without nails, with planks joined by wooden pegs and lashings, they could carry dozens of people across the seas. In 2009 a team sailed a replica balangay around Southeast Asia."],
g:[["Barangay","A village community led by a datu"],["Datu","A chief or ruler of a barangay"],["Babaylan","A spiritual leader and healer"],["Austronesian","A family of peoples and languages spread across the Pacific and Indian oceans"]],
f:["The Laguna Copperplate, from 900 CE, is the oldest known Philippine document.","The word barangay comes from balangay, a kind of boat.","Homo luzonensis lived in Cagayan at least 50,000 years ago."],
fun:"The word barangay comes from balangay, the boats in which early settlers sailed."});

ADDART({id:"baybayin",t:"Baybayin",c:"Languages",i:"✍️",w:"Baybayin",r:["phlang","precol","alphabet"],
p:[["A Philippine script","Baybayin is a writing system used in the Philippines before and during early Spanish rule."],["Syllables, not letters","Each basic symbol stands for a consonant followed by the sound a."],["Alive today","Baybayin appears on banknotes, logos and art, and related scripts are still used by Mangyan and Tagbanwa communities."]],
l:"Baybayin is a pre-colonial writing system of the Philippines, used especially by Tagalog speakers to write their language. It is an abugida, a script in which each symbol stands for a consonant with a built-in vowel. Spanish missionaries recorded that many Filipinos could read and write it. Though it fell out of everyday use, baybayin has become a proud symbol of Filipino heritage.",
s:[["How it works","Basic baybayin has 17 symbols: three vowels and 14 consonants, each consonant pronounced with the vowel a, such as ba, ka and da. A mark called a kudlit placed above changes the vowel to e or i, and one placed below changes it to o or u. Words are written as a string of syllables."],["The name","Baybayin comes from the Tagalog word baybay, meaning to spell. It is sometimes called alibata, a name invented in 1914 by Paul Versoza from the first letters of the Arabic alphabet; scholars prefer baybayin."],["Spanish records","Early Spanish writers were surprised to find that many Filipinos, both men and women, could read and write. The Doctrina Christiana, printed in Manila in 1593, one of the first books printed in the Philippines, contains prayers in Spanish and in Tagalog written in both baybayin and Latin letters."],["Decline","Under Spanish rule, the Latin alphabet gradually replaced baybayin for most purposes. By the 1700s it was rarely used in the lowlands. Because it was often written on bamboo and leaves, which decay quickly, few old examples survive."]],
k:[["Type","Abugida, a syllabic script"],["Basic symbols","17: 3 vowels, 14 consonants"],["Vowel marks","Kudlit"],["Early printed example","Doctrina Christiana, 1593"],["Related living scripts","Hanunó’o, Buhid, Tagbanwa, Pala’wan"]],
d:[["1593","The Doctrina Christiana is printed with Tagalog in baybayin"],["1620","Francisco López adds a cross-shaped kudlit to cancel the vowel sound"],["1914","The name alibata is coined"],["1999","Four Philippine scripts are added to UNESCO’s Memory of the World register"],["2010","Baybayin appears on the New Generation Philippine banknotes"]],
s2:[["Cousins in Southeast Asia","Baybayin belongs to a family of scripts descended from the Brahmi script of ancient India, which spread through Southeast Asia. Its relatives include old scripts of Java, Sulawesi and Sumatra. Other Philippine languages, such as Kapampangan, Ilocano and Visayan languages, had their own versions."],["Scripts still in use","The Hanunó’o and Buhid Mangyan of Mindoro and the Tagbanwa and Pala’wan of Palawan still use related scripts. The Hanunó’o carve poems called ambahan into bamboo tubes. In 1999 these four scripts were inscribed in UNESCO’s Memory of the World register as Philippine Paleographs."],["The López kudlit","Because baybayin could not show a consonant without a vowel, words such as bundok had to be written as bu-do. In 1620 the Spanish friar Francisco López introduced a cross-shaped kudlit to cancel the vowel. Many modern writers use it, though it was not part of the original script."],["Revival","Since the late 1900s, interest in baybayin has grown. Artists, designers and tattoo artists use it, and it appears in government seals and on Philippine banknotes, where the word Pilipino is written in baybayin. Schools and online courses teach it, and some bills in Congress have proposed making it a national writing system."],["Writing your name","To write a name in baybayin, break it into syllables. For example, Maria becomes ma-ri-ya, written with the symbols for ma, ra with a kudlit above for ri, and ya. Some sounds, such as f and v, had no symbols, so modern writers adapt the script or use the nearest sound."],["The Calatagan Pot","A clay pot found at Calatagan, Batangas, in 1958 has an inscription around its rim in a baybayin-like script. Its writing has not been fully deciphered, and scholars still debate its age and meaning. It is kept at the National Museum of the Philippines."]],
sp:["The ambahan","The Hanunó’o Mangyan of Mindoro write ambahan, poems of seven-syllable lines, on bamboo using a related script carved with a knife. Ambahan are recited or chanted to give advice, express love or teach. Young people learn the script so they can read and write ambahan, keeping one of the oldest Philippine writing traditions alive."],
g:[["Abugida","A script in which each symbol is a consonant with a built-in vowel"],["Kudlit","A mark that changes the vowel sound of a baybayin symbol"],["Script","A system of written symbols"],["Ambahan","A Mangyan poem often written on bamboo"]],
f:["Baybayin comes from the Tagalog word baybay, to spell.","The name alibata was only invented in 1914.","Philippine banknotes show the word Pilipino in baybayin."],
fun:"The word Pilipino is written in baybayin on Philippine banknotes."});

ADDART({id:"indig",t:"Indigenous Peoples of the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"🪶",w:"Indigenous peoples of the Philippines",r:["banaue","phbio","phlang"],
p:[["Many peoples","More than 100 Indigenous groups live in the Philippines, from the mountains of the Cordillera to the forests of Mindanao."],["Living traditions","They keep their own languages, beliefs, crafts, music and ways of caring for the land."],["Rights by law","The Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act of 1997 recognizes their rights to their ancestral lands and cultures."]],
l:"Indigenous peoples are communities whose cultures and ways of life continued with little change through colonial times. The Philippines is home to more than 100 Indigenous groups, numbering an estimated 14 to 17 million people. From the rice-terrace builders of the Cordillera to the Lumad of Mindanao, they carry knowledge, arts and traditions thousands of years old.",
s:[["Who they are","Indigenous Filipinos include the Igorot peoples of the Cordillera, such as the Ifugao, Kalinga, Bontoc, Kankanaey and Ibaloi; the Aeta and other Negrito groups of Luzon and the Visayas; the Mangyan of Mindoro; the Tagbanwa, Pala’wan and Batak of Palawan; and the Lumad of Mindanao, including the Manobo, T’boli, Bagobo and Subanen."],["Land and life","Most Indigenous communities are closely tied to their ancestral lands, which provide food, medicine and sacred places. Many practice rice-terrace farming, shifting cultivation, hunting, fishing and gathering forest products, guided by customary laws and elders."],["Arts and crafts","Indigenous Filipinos are known for fine weaving, such as the T’boli t’nalak made from abaca fiber, Ifugao woodcarving, Kalinga tattooing, Maranao-style metalwork and beadwork. Their gongs, bamboo instruments, dances and epics are treasured parts of Philippine culture."],["The law","The Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act of 1997 recognizes Indigenous peoples’ rights to their ancestral domains, self-governance, cultural integrity and free and prior informed consent before projects on their lands. It created the National Commission on Indigenous Peoples to protect these rights."]],
k:[["Groups","More than 100"],["Estimated population","About 14–17 million"],["Key law","Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act, 1997"],["Government agency","National Commission on Indigenous Peoples"],["World Heritage","Rice Terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras, 1995"]],
d:[["1992","Law creates the GAMABA, the National Living Treasures Award"],["1995","The Ifugao rice terraces become a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["1997","The Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act becomes law"],["2001","The Hudhud chants of the Ifugao are recognized by UNESCO"],["2023","Kalinga tattoo artist Apo Whang-od appears on the cover of Vogue Philippines"]],
s2:[["The Igorot of the Cordillera","The peoples of the Cordillera mountains of northern Luzon resisted Spanish conquest for centuries. They built the famous rice terraces, kept their own laws and beliefs, and are known for their woven textiles, woodcarving and gong music. Igorot is a general name, and many prefer to be called by their specific group."],["The Aeta","The Aeta and related groups, such as the Agta and Ati, are among the earliest inhabitants of the Philippines. Many live in the mountains of Zambales, Pampanga and Tarlac. The 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo destroyed many Aeta villages and forced thousands to move."],["The Lumad","Lumad is a Cebuano word meaning native, used for the non-Muslim Indigenous peoples of Mindanao. There are about 18 Lumad groups. Many live in mountains rich in forests and minerals, and some communities have faced conflict, displacement and threats over their land."],["Living treasures","The Gawad sa Manlilikha ng Bayan, or National Living Treasures Award, honors traditional artists who keep crafts alive. Awardees include T’boli weaver Lang Dulay and Hanunó’o Mangyan poet Ginaw Bilog. Kalinga tattoo artist Apo Whang-od, whose hand-tapped tattoos draw visitors from around the world, has also been widely honored."],["The Hudhud","The Hudhud is a long chanted epic of the Ifugao, performed during rice harvests, funerals and wakes. A single performance can take several days. UNESCO recognized it as a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2001. Few young people now learn it, and schools of living tradition work to teach them."],["Challenges and change","Indigenous Filipinos often face poverty, poor access to schools and health care, and pressure on their lands from mining, logging, dams and plantations. At the same time, many young Indigenous people are reviving their languages and crafts, and Indigenous knowledge of forests and farming is valued in caring for the environment."]],
sp:["Apo Whang-od","Maria Oggay, known as Apo Whang-od, is a Kalinga tattoo artist from Buscalan who has tapped traditional tattoos by hand with a thorn and soot ink for about 90 years. Once given to warriors and women, these tattoos are now sought by visitors from around the world. In 2023, aged about 106, she appeared on the cover of Vogue Philippines."],
g:[["Indigenous","Belonging to the original peoples of a place"],["Ancestral domain","Land that a community has held since time immemorial"],["Lumad","A Cebuano word for the non-Muslim Indigenous peoples of Mindanao"],["T’nalak","A sacred abaca cloth woven by the T’boli"]],
f:["More than 100 Indigenous groups live in the Philippines.","The Hudhud chants can take days to perform.","Apo Whang-od has been tattooing for about 90 years."],
fun:"T’boli weavers say the patterns of t’nalak cloth come to them in their dreams."});

ADDART({id:"martiallaw",t:"Martial Law in the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"⚖️",w:"Martial law under Ferdinand Marcos",r:["marcos","ninoy","edsa"],
p:[["Military rule","On 21 September 1972, President Ferdinand Marcos placed the Philippines under martial law."],["Rule by decree","Congress was closed, opponents were arrested and the press was controlled."],["Lasting debate","Martial law was formally lifted in 1981, but Marcos held power until the People Power Revolution of 1986."]],
l:"Martial law in the Philippines refers to the period of military rule declared by President Ferdinand Marcos in 1972. Marcos said it was needed to stop communist rebellion and disorder. During martial law, he ruled by decree, closed Congress, controlled the media and jailed opponents, and thousands of people suffered human rights abuses. It remains one of the most important and most debated chapters in Philippine history.",
s:[["The declaration","Proclamation No. 1081, dated 21 September 1972, placed the whole country under martial law; Marcos announced it on television on 23 September. Marcos, elected in 1965 and reelected in 1969, was nearing the end of the two terms allowed by the 1935 Constitution. He cited bombings, a reported ambush of his defense secretary and the growing communist insurgency."],["Closing democracy","Soldiers closed Congress and newspapers, radio and television stations. Opposition leaders, including Senator Benigno Ninoy Aquino Jr., journalists and student activists were arrested. A curfew was imposed, and public protests were banned. A new constitution, ratified in 1973 through citizens’ assemblies, kept Marcos in power."],["Human rights abuses","Many people were imprisoned without trial, tortured, killed or made to disappear. Historian Alfred McCoy, citing Amnesty International and other sources, estimated about 70,000 imprisonments, 34,000 cases of torture and 3,257 killings. A 2013 law later recognized more than 11,000 victims and their families for reparations."],["Lifting and after","Martial law was formally lifted on 17 January 1981, but Marcos kept the power to make laws by decree. After Ninoy Aquino was assassinated on his return from exile in August 1983, public anger grew. In February 1986, after a disputed snap election, the People Power Revolution forced Marcos into exile in Hawaii."]],
k:[["Declared","21 September 1972, Proclamation No. 1081"],["Lifted","17 January 1981"],["President","Ferdinand E. Marcos"],["Recognized victims","More than 11,000 (Republic Act 10368)"],["Ended by","People Power Revolution, February 1986"]],
d:[["1972","Martial law is declared"],["1973","A new constitution is ratified"],["1981","Martial law is formally lifted"],["1983","Ninoy Aquino is assassinated at Manila International Airport"],["2013","The Human Rights Victims Reparation and Recognition Act is passed"]],
s2:[["The economy","In the early years of martial law, the economy grew and the government built roads, bridges, power plants, hospitals and cultural centers, and expanded rice production through the Masagana 99 program. Later, heavy borrowing, falling export prices and favoritism toward businessmen close to the Marcos family led to a severe crisis. Foreign debt rose to about US$26 billion by 1986."],["Cronyism and ill-gotten wealth","Close allies of Marcos, known as cronies, were given control of major industries such as sugar, coconuts and media. After 1986 the Presidential Commission on Good Government was created to recover wealth said to have been taken from the nation, and courts have ordered the return of billions of pesos, including Swiss bank deposits."],["Resistance","Students, workers, farmers, church groups, journalists and politicians resisted martial law through protests, underground newspapers and elections. Many paid with their freedom or their lives. Their courage helped build the movement that led to the People Power Revolution in 1986."],["Remembering the victims","The Bantayog ng mga Bayani in Quezon City honors people who fought the dictatorship, with their names carved on a Wall of Remembrance. Republic Act 10368 of 2013 recognized the victims of human rights violations and provided reparations funded partly by recovered Marcos wealth."],["Continuing debate","Supporters of Marcos remember the period for its infrastructure, peace and order and national discipline. Critics, historians and victims emphasize the abuses, the loss of democracy, corruption and the debts left behind. The election of Marcos’s son, Ferdinand Marcos Jr., as president in 2022 renewed public debate about how the period is remembered and taught."],["Safeguards since 1986","The 1987 Constitution limits martial law to 60 days, requires the president to report to Congress within 48 hours and allows Congress to revoke it and the Supreme Court to review it. These rules were applied when martial law was declared in Mindanao in 2017 during the Battle of Marawi."]],
sp:["The radio that would not stop","When martial law closed most news outlets, the Catholic station Radio Veritas and underground newspapers such as Ang Pahayagang Malaya and the mimeographed sheets of student groups kept spreading news. In February 1986, Radio Veritas called people to EDSA to protect soldiers who had broken away from Marcos, helping start the People Power Revolution."],
g:[["Martial law","Military rule that replaces normal civilian government"],["Decree","An order with the force of law made by a ruler"],["Curfew","A rule requiring people to stay indoors at certain times"],["Reparations","Payments made to repair a wrong"]],
f:["Martial law was declared in 1972 and formally lifted in 1981.","More than 11,000 victims were recognized under a 2013 law.","The 1987 Constitution limits martial law to 60 days."],
fun:"The 1987 Constitution limits any declaration of martial law to 60 days unless Congress extends it."});

ADDART({id:"ofw",t:"Filipinos Overseas",c:"Philippines",i:"✈️",w:"Overseas Filipinos",r:["phecon","labor","trade"],
p:[["A global people","About 10 million Filipinos live or work outside the Philippines."],["Overseas Filipino workers","Around 2 million work abroad at any one time as nurses, seafarers, caregivers, engineers and domestic workers."],["Remittances","Money sent home by Filipinos abroad supports millions of families and a large part of the economy."]],
l:"Filipinos are one of the world’s great migrant peoples. Millions live permanently in other countries, and about 2 million work abroad on contracts at any one time as overseas Filipino workers, or OFWs. The money they send home supports families, schools and businesses, while their absence also brings sacrifice. Filipinos abroad have shaped communities from the Gulf to North America.",
s:[["Where they go","The most common destinations for OFWs include Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait, Qatar, Hong Kong, Singapore and Taiwan. Many Filipinos have settled permanently in the United States, Canada, Australia, Japan and Europe. There are about 4 million people of Filipino heritage in the United States alone."],["What they do","Filipinos abroad work in hospitals, ships, homes, hotels, construction sites, offices and factories. The Philippines is one of the world’s largest sources of nurses and caregivers, and Filipino seafarers make up a large share of the crews on the world’s merchant ships."],["Remittances","Money sent home by overseas Filipinos reached about US$38 billion in 2024, about 8 percent of the country’s economy. Remittances pay for food, schooling, medical care and houses, and help keep the peso stable. December, when families prepare for Christmas, is usually the busiest month for remittances."],["A government policy","The government began organizing overseas employment in 1974 under the Labor Code, intending it as a temporary solution to unemployment. It grew into a permanent part of the economy. In 2021 Congress created the Department of Migrant Workers to protect and support OFWs."]],
k:[["Filipinos overseas","About 10 million"],["OFWs at any time","About 2 million"],["Remittances","About US$38 billion in 2024"],["Government department","Department of Migrant Workers, since 2021"],["Migrant Workers Day","7 June"]],
d:[["1974","The Labor Code launches the overseas employment program"],["1995","The execution of Flor Contemplacion in Singapore sparks national outrage"],["1995","The Migrant Workers and Overseas Filipinos Act is passed"],["2013","The Overseas Absentee Voting law is expanded to all citizens abroad"],["2021","The Department of Migrant Workers is created"]],
s2:[["Bagong bayani","Overseas workers are often called bagong bayani, new heroes, for the sacrifices they make for their families and country. Each December, airports hold special welcomes for returning OFWs. The government gives awards to outstanding migrant workers and their families."],["The balikbayan box","The balikbayan box is a large cardboard box filled with gifts, clothes, food and household goods that overseas Filipinos send home. Families often fill a box slowly over months. Shipping companies carry hundreds of thousands of balikbayan boxes to the Philippines every year, especially before Christmas."],["Families apart","Working abroad often means years away from spouses, children and parents. Many children grow up with one or both parents overseas, keeping in touch by video calls. Grandparents and relatives often help raise them. The social costs of migration are a frequent subject of Filipino films and teleseryes."],["Dangers and protection","Some OFWs, especially domestic workers, face abuse, unpaid wages, confiscated passports or illegal recruiters. The 1995 execution of Flor Contemplacion in Singapore led to the Migrant Workers Act. The government sets rules for recruitment agencies, signs agreements with host countries and keeps labor offices in many embassies."],["Seafarers","Filipino seafarers are among the most numerous in the world’s merchant fleets, working on cargo ships, tankers and cruise ships. Their skill in English and their training have made them sought after. Maritime schools across the Philippines train thousands of new seafarers each year."],["A global community","Filipino communities abroad build churches, start restaurants, hold fiestas and celebrate Independence Day with parades, such as the Philippine Independence Day Parade in New York. Overseas Filipinos can vote in national elections, and many return home to retire or invest."]],
sp:["Flor Contemplacion","Flor Contemplacion was a Filipina domestic worker in Singapore who was convicted of murder and executed in March 1995, despite appeals from the Philippine government and doubts raised about the case. Her death caused nationwide grief and anger, strained relations with Singapore and led Congress to pass the Migrant Workers and Overseas Filipinos Act that year."],
g:[["OFW","Overseas Filipino worker, employed abroad on contract"],["Remittance","Money sent home by someone working away"],["Balikbayan","A Filipino returning home from abroad"],["Seafarer","A person who works on a ship"]],
f:["About 10 million Filipinos live or work abroad.","Remittances reached about US$38 billion in 2024.","June 7 is Migrant Workers Day in the Philippines."],
fun:"Balikbayan boxes are often filled slowly over many months before being shipped home."});

ADDART({id:"tubbataha",t:"Tubbataha Reefs",c:"Philippines",i:"🐠",w:"Tubbataha Reef",r:["reef","palawan","seaturtle"],
p:[["A jewel of the Sulu Sea","Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park protects two huge coral atolls in the middle of the Sulu Sea."],["World Heritage","It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1993."],["Teeming with life","Its waters hold hundreds of kinds of coral and fish, sharks, turtles and seabirds."]],
l:"Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park is a marine protected area in the middle of the Sulu Sea, about 150 kilometers southeast of Puerto Princesa, Palawan. Its two coral atolls and the Jessie Beazley Reef are among the healthiest and most diverse coral reefs in the world. A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1993, Tubbataha is guarded by rangers all year round.",
s:[["Location","Tubbataha lies in the Sulu Sea and belongs to the municipality of Cagayancillo, Palawan. It is far from any large island, which helps keep it pristine. The park covers about 97,000 hectares of sea, including the North and South Atolls and Jessie Beazley Reef."],["Coral atolls","An atoll is a ring of coral reef surrounding a lagoon. Tubbataha’s atolls sit on top of ancient volcanic peaks under the sea. Their reef walls drop steeply into deep blue water, more than 100 meters down, making the park one of the world’s great diving sites."],["Marine life","Tubbataha is home to about 600 species of fish, about 360 species of coral, about half of all coral species known in the world, and many sharks, rays, dolphins and whales. Hawksbill and green sea turtles nest on its small islands, and thousands of seabirds breed on Bird Islet."],["Protection","Tubbataha became the Philippines’ first national marine park in 1988. Fishing and collecting are banned. Park rangers from the navy, coast guard and local government live at a ranger station on the reef year-round, guarding against illegal fishing. The Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park Act of 2010 strengthened its protection."]],
k:[["Location","Sulu Sea, Cagayancillo, Palawan"],["Area","About 97,000 hectares"],["Coral species","About 360"],["Fish species","About 600"],["World Heritage","Since 1993, extended in 2009"]],
d:[["1988","Tubbataha is declared the first national marine park in the Philippines"],["1993","UNESCO inscribes Tubbataha as a World Heritage Site"],["2009","The World Heritage Site is extended to include Jessie Beazley Reef"],["2010","The Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park Act becomes law"],["2013","The USS Guardian runs aground on the reef"]],
s2:[["Diving season","Tubbataha can only be visited by liveaboard dive boats from mid-March to mid-June, when the sea is calm. Divers come from around the world to see walls of coral, schools of jacks and barracuda, manta rays and reef sharks. Visitors pay a conservation fee that helps fund the rangers."],["Seabirds","Bird Islet and South Islet host large breeding colonies of seabirds, including brown boobies, sooty terns, noddies and the rare Christmas Island frigatebird, which visits outside its breeding season. Seabird numbers have grown since rats were removed from the islets."],["Groundings","In January 2013 a US Navy minesweeper, the USS Guardian, ran aground on Tubbataha, damaging about 2,345 square meters of reef. The ship had to be cut apart to remove it. The United States paid compensation, and the case led to stricter rules on ships entering the park."],["Healthy reefs","Scientists regularly survey Tubbataha and find it has some of the highest densities of fish in the Philippines. Its reefs supply fish larvae carried by currents to other reefs across the Sulu Sea, helping restock fishing grounds far away."],["Threats","Even remote reefs face threats from warming seas, which cause coral bleaching, plastic waste carried by currents and illegal fishing. The rangers’ long-term presence and strict protection make Tubbataha a model for marine conservation in Southeast Asia."],["Why it matters","Healthy coral reefs protect coasts from waves, provide food and income for millions of people and are home to a quarter of all ocean species. Tubbataha shows how strong protection can keep a reef thriving and benefit the wider sea."]],
sp:["The ranger station","Tubbataha’s rangers live in shifts of about two months at a time on a small station built on stilts above the reef, with only the sea, the coral and the seabirds around them. They patrol in boats day and night, check visiting vessels and record wildlife. Their dedication has helped keep Tubbataha one of the healthiest reefs on Earth."],
g:[["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral reef around a lagoon"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea by a reef"],["Coral bleaching","When stressed corals lose the algae that give them color and food"],["Marine protected area","An area of sea where activities are limited to protect nature"]],
f:["Tubbataha has about half of the world’s known coral species.","Rangers guard Tubbataha all year round.","Tubbataha became a World Heritage Site in 1993."],
fun:"Tubbataha’s reefs are home to about half of all coral species known in the world."});

ADDART({id:"apo",t:"Mount Apo",c:"Philippines",i:"⛰️",w:"Mount Apo",r:["mindanao","eagle","volc"],
p:[["The highest peak","Mount Apo, at 2,954 meters, is the highest mountain in the Philippines."],["A sleeping volcano","It is a potentially active volcano with steaming vents near its summit."],["Grandfather of mountains","Apo means elder or grandfather, and the mountain is sacred to the peoples who live around it."]],
l:"Mount Apo is the highest mountain in the Philippines, rising 2,954 meters above sea level in Mindanao, on the border of Davao City, Davao del Sur and Cotabato. A large, potentially active volcano with sulfur vents near its summit, it is covered in rainforest and mossy forest that shelter the Philippine eagle and thousands of other species. Its name means elder or grandfather.",
s:[["The mountain","Mount Apo is a stratovolcano, built from layers of lava and ash. It has no recorded eruptions, but steam and sulfur gases still rise from vents called solfataras near the summit, and hot springs flow on its slopes. PHIVOLCS classifies it as potentially active."],["Forests and wildlife","As you climb, lowland rainforest gives way to cooler montane forest and then to mossy forest, where trees are draped in moss, ferns and orchids. The mountain is home to the Philippine eagle, tarsiers, deer and many birds, insects and plants found nowhere else, including the waling-waling orchid."],["Protected park","Mount Apo was made a national park in 1936, one of the oldest in the country. The Mount Apo Protected Area Act of 2004 strengthened its protection. It is also an ASEAN Heritage Park. Climbers must get permits and follow rules to protect its fragile environment."],["Sacred mountain","For the Bagobo, Manobo and other Indigenous peoples of the area, Mount Apo is a sacred place, home of spirits and ancestors. Some call it Sandawa. Indigenous communities help manage the park and serve as guides and porters for climbers."]],
k:[["Height","2,954 m"],["Location","Davao City, Davao del Sur and Cotabato, Mindanao"],["Type","Stratovolcano, potentially active"],["National park","Since 1936"],["Meaning of name","Elder or grandfather"]],
d:[["1859","A Spanish-led party makes the first recorded climb of Mount Apo"],["1936","Mount Apo National Park is established"],["1984","Mount Apo becomes an ASEAN Heritage Park"],["2004","The Mount Apo Protected Area Act becomes law"],["2016","A forest fire burns part of the summit area, and trails are closed for recovery"]],
s2:[["Climbing Apo","Climbing Mount Apo usually takes two to four days. Popular trails start from Kapatagan in Davao del Sur, Kidapawan in Cotabato and Santa Cruz. Climbers pass forests, rivers, boulder fields and steaming vents before reaching the summit, where on clear days they can see Davao Gulf and the surrounding mountains."],["The first climb","The first recorded climb of Mount Apo was in October 1859, by a party led by Don José Oyanguren, with Bagobo guides. Earlier attempts had failed because of the thick forest, steep slopes and sulfur fumes. Today thousands of climbers reach the summit each year."],["The Philippine eagle","The forests of Mount Apo are one of the strongholds of the Philippine eagle, the national bird. The Philippine Eagle Center in Davao City, near the mountain, breeds eagles and teaches visitors about protecting them and their forests."],["Geothermal power","Heat from deep inside the volcano is used to make electricity at geothermal power plants on Mount Apo’s slopes. Hot water and steam from underground turn turbines, supplying power to Mindanao without burning fuel."],["Fire and recovery","In March 2016, during a drought caused by El Niño, a forest fire burned a large area near the summit. The mountain was closed to climbers for many months so the forest could recover. Rangers and Indigenous volunteers helped fight the fire and replant trees."],["Durian and coffee","The fertile volcanic soil around Mount Apo grows fruits and crops for which Davao is famous, including durian, pomelo, cacao and coffee. Davao chocolate made from local cacao has won international awards."]],
sp:["Apo Sandawa","Many Indigenous peoples around the mountain call it Apo Sandawa. In their stories, the mountain is the dwelling of powerful spirits and the resting place of ancestors. Before climbing, some guides make offerings and ask permission. Their traditions remind climbers that Mount Apo is not only a challenge but a sacred home to be respected."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A tall, cone-shaped volcano built from layers of lava and ash"],["Solfatara","A volcanic vent that releases steam and sulfur gases"],["Mossy forest","A cool, misty forest where trees are covered in moss"],["Geothermal","Using heat from inside the Earth"]],
f:["Mount Apo is the highest mountain in the Philippines.","Apo means elder or grandfather.","Steam and sulfur still rise from vents near its summit."],
fun:"Apo, the name of the country’s highest mountain, means elder or grandfather."});

ADDART({id:"phgov",t:"Government of the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"🏛️",w:"Government of the Philippines",r:["independence","martiallaw","democ"],
p:[["A democratic republic","The Philippines is a democratic republic with a president elected by the people."],["Three branches","Power is divided among the executive, the legislature and the judiciary."],["The 1987 Constitution","The country’s highest law was ratified by the people in 1987, after the People Power Revolution."]],
l:"The Philippines is a democratic and republican state. Its government follows the 1987 Constitution, written after the People Power Revolution, which divides power among three branches: an executive led by the president, a two-chamber Congress that makes laws, and a judiciary headed by the Supreme Court. Local governments, from provinces down to barangays, run day-to-day affairs.",
s:[["The Constitution","The 1987 Constitution was ratified in a plebiscite on 2 February 1987. It has a preamble and 18 articles, including a Bill of Rights. It created safeguards against dictatorship, such as limits on martial law and a single six-year term for the president. Earlier constitutions were adopted in 1899, 1935 and 1973."],["The executive","The president is head of state and head of government and commands the armed forces. The president and vice president are elected separately by direct vote for six-year terms; the president cannot be reelected. The president appoints department secretaries, who form the Cabinet. As of 2026 the president is Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr., elected in 2022."],["Congress","Congress has two chambers. The Senate has 24 senators elected by the whole country for six-year terms, half every three years. The House of Representatives has more than 300 members, most elected by districts and up to 20 percent through the party-list system. Bills must pass both chambers and be signed by the president to become law."],["The judiciary","The Supreme Court, with a chief justice and 14 associate justices, is the highest court. It can strike down laws and actions that violate the Constitution. Justices are appointed by the president from lists prepared by the Judicial and Bar Council and serve until age 70."]],
k:[["Type","Democratic republic, presidential system"],["Constitution","1987"],["President’s term","6 years, no reelection"],["Senators","24"],["Supreme Court justices","15"]],
d:[["1899","The Malolos Constitution creates the First Philippine Republic"],["1935","The Commonwealth Constitution is adopted"],["1973","A new constitution is ratified under martial law"],["1987","The current constitution is ratified on 2 February"],["1991","The Local Government Code gives more power to local governments"]],
s2:[["Local government","The Philippines is divided into 82 provinces and about 150 cities, which contain more than 1,400 municipalities and about 42,000 barangays. Governors, mayors, councilors and barangay captains are elected every three years. The Local Government Code of 1991 gave local governments more powers and a share of national taxes."],["The barangay","The barangay is the smallest unit of government, led by a punong barangay, or barangay captain, and a council. Barangays keep the peace, settle small disputes through the Katarungang Pambarangay system, provide basic services and issue clearances. Young people aged 15 to 30 elect their own Sangguniang Kabataan, or youth council."],["Elections","Filipinos aged 18 and over can vote. National and local elections are held every three years in May, and the president is elected every six years. The Commission on Elections, or COMELEC, runs elections. Since 2010, votes have been counted by machines. Voter turnout is usually high."],["Checks and balances","Each branch can check the others. Congress can impeach the president and other high officials, and the Senate tries them. The Supreme Court can declare laws unconstitutional. Independent bodies, including the Commission on Audit, the Civil Service Commission, the Ombudsman and the Commission on Human Rights, watch over government."],["Autonomous region","The Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao, created in 2019 after a peace agreement, has its own parliament and a large degree of self-government over local matters. The Constitution also provides for an autonomous region in the Cordillera, though it has not yet been established."],["Changing the Constitution","The Constitution can be amended by Congress acting as a constituent assembly, by a constitutional convention or by a people’s initiative, but any change must be approved by voters in a plebiscite. Proposals to change it, often called charter change or cha-cha, have been debated many times but none has succeeded since 1987."]],
sp:["The Bill of Rights","Article III of the 1987 Constitution, the Bill of Rights, protects freedoms such as speech, the press, religion and peaceful assembly, the right to due process, protection against unreasonable searches and the right of the accused to remain silent and to have a lawyer. It also bans torture and secret detention, lessons drawn from the martial law years."],
g:[["Republic","A state in which power rests with the people and their elected representatives"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote of the people on an important question"],["Impeachment","Charging a high official with wrongdoing in order to remove them"],["Party-list","A system that gives seats in the House to groups and sectors"]],
f:["The president serves a single six-year term.","The Senate has 24 members elected by the whole country.","The barangay is the smallest unit of government."],
fun:"Filipinos aged 15 to 30 elect their own barangay youth councils, the Sangguniang Kabataan."});

